    "fate": "28.4.15 wounded at Gallipoli while serving with the 8th Infantry Battalion. 10.5.15 Died of Wounds in hospital. Place of burial: Chatby War Memorial Cemetery (Row C, Grave No. 152), Egypt. Age at death, 23 years.",
    "recognition": "1914–15 Star. British War Medal. Victory Medal. Memorial Plaque. Memorial Scroll. Smeaton State School Honour Board. Kingston State School Honour Board. RSL Virtual War Memorial. Avenue of Honour Tree E3",
    "commemoration": "Australian War Memorial Panel Number 52, Roll of Honour.",
    "warService": "Egypt",
    "nursingService": "",
    "familyConnection": "",
    "additionalNotes": "3.4.15 wrote a will leaving all to his brother George Gordon Archibald, Green Hill, Smeaton. His father received all medals, Memorial Scroll, Plaque and King’s Message.",
    "notes": "5.4.15 arrived Alexandria. 28.4.15 Wounded in Action. (Shotgun wound to head.) Admitted to 15th General Hospital, Egypt, but died from wounds on 10.5.15. Chaplin Arch Deacon Ward performed the burial rite at Chatby War Memorial Cemetery. 16.11.15 the personal effects of the late Private Archibald consisted of one box of cards, a note book and a nickel wristlet watch. These items were forwarded to his brother George in accordance with the provisions of his will. 19.1.17 Creswick Advertiser reported that he had been Killed in Action. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private Archibald from Smeaton had been a District Volunteer. 31.12.18 Creswick Advertiser published on a list of all from the Borough and Shire who had been Killed in Action including Private Archibald. Family Military Connections Brother, Private James Lindsay Archibald, [No. 677], 14th Infantry Battalion, Killed in Action France, 13.8.16. Cousin, Private Andrew Charles McKinnon [No. 323], 8th Infantry Battalion, Killed in Action France, 19.7.16.",
    "militaryMedalCitation": "",
    "fullProfile": "Archibald, Ernest Ormond Family Born Smeaton 1892. Birth Registration 1892/7960. Father: Alexander Andrew Archibald. Mother: Jessie Mann. Enlistment 22.8.14 joined Ballarat. Service number: 271. Rank: Private. Roll title: 8th Infantry Battalion (October 1914). Age on enlistment: 22 years 10 months. Occupation: Labourer (Ballarat). Marital status: Single. Religion: Presbyterian. Next of kin and address: sister, Ruby Archibald, Smeaton. Embarkation Embarked Melbourne 19.10.14 on ship A24 HMAT Benalla. Rate of pay per diem before and after embarkation: Five Shillings. Original unit: 8th Battalion, B Company. Australian War Memorial Embarkation Roll Number: 23/25/1. Later in this publication are more details about the ships that took individuals overseas. Additional Notes 3.4.15 wrote a will leaving all to his brother George Gordon Archibald, Green Hill, Smeaton. His father received all medals, Memorial Scroll, Plaque and King’s Message. Fate 28.4.15 wounded at Gallipoli while serving with the 8th Infantry Battalion. 10.5.15 Died of Wounds in hospital. Place of burial: Chatby War Memorial Cemetery (Row C, Grave No. 152), Egypt. Age at death, 23 years. Commemoration Australian War Memorial Panel Number 52, Roll of Honour. Recognition 1914–15 Star. British War Medal. Victory Medal. Memorial Plaque. Memorial Scroll. Smeaton State School Honour Board. Kingston State School Honour Board. RSL Virtual War Memorial. Avenue of Honour Tree E3 War Service Egypt Notes 5.4.15 arrived Alexandria. 28.4.15 Wounded in Action. (Shotgun wound to head.) Admitted to 15th General Hospital, Egypt, but died from wounds on 10.5.15. Chaplin Arch Deacon Ward performed the burial rite at Chatby War Memorial Cemetery. 16.11.15 the personal effects of the late Private Archibald consisted of one box of cards, a note book and a nickel wristlet watch. These items were forwarded to his brother George in accordance with the provisions of his will. 19.1.17 Creswick Advertiser reported that he had been Killed in Action. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private Archibald from Smeaton had been a District Volunteer. 31.12.18 Creswick Advertiser published on a list of all from the Borough and Shire who had been Killed in Action including Private Archibald. Family Military Connections Brother, Private James Lindsay Archibald, [No. 677], 14th Infantry Battalion, Killed in Action France, 13.8.16. Cousin, Private Andrew Charles McKinnon [No. 323], 8th Infantry Battalion, Killed in Action France, 19.7.16.",
