    "service": "Joined 29.8.15 Blackboy Hill WA. Service number: 3243. Rank: Private. Roll title: 11th Infantry Battalion, 8th to 12th Reinforcements, (September to November 1915). Age on enlistment: 31 years 5 months. Marital status: Married. Religion: Church of England. Occupation: carpenter. Next of kin and address: wife, Mrs. Fanny Armstrong, Cranbrook, Western Australia. Embarked Fremantle 2.11.15, on ship A38 HMAT Ulysses. Rate of pay per diem before and after embarkation: Five Shillings. Original unit: 11th Battalion, 11th Reinforcement. Australian War Memorial Embarkation Roll Number: 23/28/3. Later in this publication are more details about the ships that took individuals overseas.",
    "family": "Born Creswick 1883. Birth Registration 1883/8431. Father: Thomas Henry Armstrong. Mother: Annie Robins. Married Fanny Gardiner, Katanning Western Australia 8.2.1910. Marriage Registration 1911/21.",
    "enlistment": "Joined 29.8.15 Blackboy Hill WA. Service number: 3243. Rank: Private. Roll title: 11th Infantry Battalion, 8th to 12th Reinforcements, (September to November 1915). Age on enlistment: 31 years 5 months. Marital status: Married. Religion: Church of England. Occupation: carpenter. Next of kin and address: wife, Mrs. Fanny Armstrong, Cranbrook, Western Australia.",
    "embarkation": "Embarked Fremantle 2.11.15, on ship A38 HMAT Ulysses. Rate of pay per diem before and after embarkation: Five Shillings. Original unit: 11th Battalion, 11th Reinforcement. Australian War Memorial Embarkation Roll Number: 23/28/3. Later in this publication are more details about the ships that took individuals overseas.",
    "fate": "14.5.19 returned to Australia on board the ship Trasos Montes. 2.6.19 discharged 5th Military District. Died 23.10.1952 Katanning, WA. Age at death, 68 years. Death Registration 1950/102.",
    "recognition": "Military Medal. 1914–15 Star. British War Medal. Victory Medal. RSL Virtual War Memorial. Avenue of Honour Tree W93 Military Medal Citation: “In the operations against the enemy positions at MONT DE MERRIS near STRAZEELE on night 2nd /3rd June 1918, Corporal ARMSTRONG did exceptionally gallant work in installing and maintaining telephonic communication. He accompanied the attacking troops on right flank to both first and second objectives and at the same time laid a cable, with the result that within a very short time after the positions were captured Companies were in direct communication with Battalion headquarters. When a forward line was cut this NCO crawled out across country under observation of enemy snipers and succeeded in repairing same.”",
    "commemoration": "",
    "warService": "",
    "nursingService": "",
    "familyConnection": "Brother, Private Francis Aubrey Armstrong, [No. 6471], 16th Infantry Battalion, returned to Australia 28.3.19.",
    "additionalNotes": "Second name also spelt Leycester or Lester.",
    "notes": "Private from 2.9.15; Acting Corporal from 28.10.15 to 2.3.16. 15.11.17; appointed Lance Corporal; 24.6.18 promoted to Sergeant; 21.3.19 promoted to Warrant Officer class 1. 23.7.16 Wounded in Action France. (Gunshot wound to face.) Admitted to 5th Southern General Hospital Portsmouth, England after being evacuated from Le Havre, France on board hospital ship Asturias. 13.10.16 Creswick Advertiser reported that he had been Wounded in Action. 26.4.17 to 9.6.17 qualified as assistant instructor at the Southern Command Signal School Weymouth, England. Recommended for Distinguished Conduct Medal (altered to Military Medal) June 1918. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private Armstrong from Rocklyn had been a District Volunteer. 31.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported on all from the Borough and Shire who had been Wounded in Action during WW1, including Corporal Armstrong.",
    "militaryMedalCitation": "",
    "fullProfile": "Armstrong, Wilfred Leicester Gordon Family Born Creswick 1883. Birth Registration 1883/8431. Father: Thomas Henry Armstrong. Mother: Annie Robins. Married Fanny Gardiner, Katanning Western Australia 8.2.1910. Marriage Registration 1911/21. Enlistment Joined 29.8.15 Blackboy Hill WA. Service number: 3243. Rank: Private. Roll title: 11th Infantry Battalion, 8th to 12th Reinforcements, (September to November 1915). Age on enlistment: 31 years 5 months. Marital status: Married. Religion: Church of England. Occupation: carpenter. Next of kin and address: wife, Mrs. Fanny Armstrong, Cranbrook, Western Australia. Embarkation Embarked Fremantle 2.11.15, on ship A38 HMAT Ulysses. Rate of pay per diem before and after embarkation: Five Shillings. Original unit: 11th Battalion, 11th Reinforcement. Australian War Memorial Embarkation Roll Number: 23/28/3. Later in this publication are more details about the ships that took individuals overseas. Additional Notes Second name also spelt Leycester or Lester. Fate 14.5.19 returned to Australia on board the ship Trasos Montes. 2.6.19 discharged 5th Military District. Died 23.10.1952 Katanning, WA. Age at death, 68 years. Death Registration 1950/102. Recognition Military Medal. 1914–15 Star. British War Medal. Victory Medal. RSL Virtual War Memorial. Avenue of Honour Tree W93 Military Medal Citation: “In the operations against the enemy positions at MONT DE MERRIS near STRAZEELE on night 2nd /3rd June 1918, Corporal ARMSTRONG did exceptionally gallant work in installing and maintaining telephonic communication. He accompanied the attacking troops on right flank to both first and second objectives and at the same time laid a cable, with the result that within a very short time after the positions were captured Companies were in direct communication with Battalion headquarters. When a forward line was cut this NCO crawled out across country under observation of enemy snipers and succeeded in repairing same.” Notes Private from 2.9.15; Acting Corporal from 28.10.15 to 2.3.16. 15.11.17; appointed Lance Corporal; 24.6.18 promoted to Sergeant; 21.3.19 promoted to Warrant Officer class 1. 23.7.16 Wounded in Action France. (Gunshot wound to face.) Admitted to 5th Southern General Hospital Portsmouth, England after being evacuated from Le Havre, France on board hospital ship Asturias. 13.10.16 Creswick Advertiser reported that he had been Wounded in Action. 26.4.17 to 9.6.17 qualified as assistant instructor at the Southern Command Signal School Weymouth, England. Recommended for Distinguished Conduct Medal (altered to Military Medal) June 1918. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private Armstrong from Rocklyn had been a District Volunteer. 31.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported on all from the Borough and Shire who had been Wounded in Action during WW1, including Corporal Armstrong. Family Military Connection Brother, Private Francis Aubrey Armstrong, [No. 6471], 16th Infantry Battalion, returned to Australia 28.3.19.",
