    "additionalNotes": "Birth registered at Mount Prospect. Family Military Connections Brother, Private Albert Victor Atkins, [No. 4734], 5th Infantry Battalion, returned to Australia 24.7.19. Brother, Driver John Gordon Atkins, [No. 8735], 2nd Divisional Ammunition Column, returned to Australia 3.5.19. Brother, Private Nathaniel Bryan Atkins, [No. 4557], 24th Infantry Battalion, returned to Australia 7.10.19. Brother, Private Silas Atkins [No. 2952], 8th Infantry Battalion, returned to Australia 25.1.19.",
    "notes": "11.11.16 arrived Plymouth, England. 16.11.16 marched into 3rd Amalgamated Training Depot Hurdcott England. 12.9.17 admitted to 10th Australian Field Ambulance, France with PUO (pyrexia of unknown origin). Referred to 59th General Hospital. 13.10.17 admitted to 4th Stationary Hospital Anques. Medical Report September 1917 reported three episodes of trench fever. 28.2.18 admitted to 2nd General Hospitalised Le Havre, France with dacryocystitis. 25.3.18 evacuated to England for treatment on board hospital ship Panama. 28.3.18 admitted to King George Hospital Stamford Street London with dacryocystitis. 13.9.18 proceeded overseas to France to re-join 38th Battalion in the field. 8.2.19 Medical Report declared that Private Atkins was suffering from debility brought on by a number of bouts of trench fever and relapses. Some recurrence of pain since leaving the front line. No details on file concerning the granting of a pension.",
    "militaryMedalCitation": "",
    "fullProfile": "Atkins, William Henry Family Born Newlyn 1880. Birth Registration 1880/10491. Father: William Henry Atkins. Mother: Elizabeth May Johnson. Married Cecilia Victoria Abbott. Marriage Registration 1902/7002. Second Marriage to Eileen Elizabeth Alice Jackson 1940. Marriage Registration 1940/16689. Enlistment Joined 5.4.16 Horsham. Service number: 2034. Rank: Private. Roll title: 38th Infantry Battalion, 1st to 6th Reinforcements, (June to December 1916). Age on enlistment: 35 years 8 months. Occupation: Skilled Labourer (Horsham). Marital status: Married. Religion: Church of Christ. Next of kin and address: wife, Mrs. Cecilia Atkins, Darlot Street Horsham. Embarkation Embarked Melbourne 25.9.16 on ship A9 HMAT Shropshire. Rate of pay per diem before and after embarkation: Five Shillings. Original unit: 38th Battalion, 3rd Reinforcement. Australian War Memorial Embarkation Roll Number: 23/55/2. Later in this publication are more details about the ships that took individuals overseas. Fate 12.5.19 returned to Australia on board the ship Soudan. 6.8.19 discharged 3rd Military District as medically unfit. Died 1968 Horsham. Age at death, 87 years. Death Registration 1968/6609. Place of burial: Horsham Cemetery. Recognition British War. Victory Medal. RSL Virtual War Memorial. Avenue of Honour Tree E122 Additional Notes Birth registered at Mount Prospect. Family Military Connections Brother, Private Albert Victor Atkins, [No. 4734], 5th Infantry Battalion, returned to Australia 24.7.19. Brother, Driver John Gordon Atkins, [No. 8735], 2nd Divisional Ammunition Column, returned to Australia 3.5.19. Brother, Private Nathaniel Bryan Atkins, [No. 4557], 24th Infantry Battalion, returned to Australia 7.10.19. Brother, Private Silas Atkins [No. 2952], 8th Infantry Battalion, returned to Australia 25.1.19. Notes 11.11.16 arrived Plymouth, England. 16.11.16 marched into 3rd Amalgamated Training Depot Hurdcott England. 12.9.17 admitted to 10th Australian Field Ambulance, France with PUO (pyrexia of unknown origin). Referred to 59th General Hospital. 13.10.17 admitted to 4th Stationary Hospital Anques. Medical Report September 1917 reported three episodes of trench fever. 28.2.18 admitted to 2nd General Hospitalised Le Havre, France with dacryocystitis. 25.3.18 evacuated to England for treatment on board hospital ship Panama. 28.3.18 admitted to King George Hospital Stamford Street London with dacryocystitis. 13.9.18 proceeded overseas to France to re-join 38th Battalion in the field. 8.2.19 Medical Report declared that Private Atkins was suffering from debility brought on by a number of bouts of trench fever and relapses. Some recurrence of pain since leaving the front line. No details on file concerning the granting of a pension.",
