    "additionalNotes": "3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private Baker from Smeaton had been a District Volunteer. Driver from 9.6.16; Corporal from 12.1.17; Acting Staff Sergeant when mentioned in Despatches.",
    "notes": "10.10.16 Acting Sergeant Camel Corps while en route by ship to Middle East. 9.11.16 taken on strength with 3rd Light Horse Training Regiment. 31.1.17 transferred to AIF Headquarters in Cairo from 11th Light Horse Regiment. Posted for duty with Australian Army Pay Corps. 13.6.17 while on active service, forfeited 2 days’ pay for failing to salute an officer in Cairo. 3.10.18 admitted to hospital for disease – remained 37 days before discharge. Mentioned in Despatches, Awarded and Promulgated London Gazette No. 31383, dated 5.6.1919, and Commonwealth Gazette No. 113, dated 6.10.1919. 14.11.19 AIF Headquarters Melbourne notified Trooper Baker that he had been Mentioned in Despatches by General Allenby, Commander in Chief, Egyptian Expeditionary Force, “for conspicuous service rendered on behalf of the AIF”. 28.10.20 Having been Mentioned in Despatches twice, he received two Oak Leaves (one large and one small), to be worn with other Military decorations.",
    "militaryMedalCitation": "",
    "fullProfile": "Baker, John Alfred Family Born Ballarat 1883. Birth Registration 1883/13903. Father: John Alfred Baker. Mother: Martha Lynch Douglas. Enlistment Joined 5.5.16 Rockhampton Queensland. Service number: 2016. Rank: Private. Roll title: 11th Light Horse Regiment, 13th Reinforcement. Age on enlistment: 32 years 9 months. Occupation: Bookkeeper. Marital status: Single. Religion: Presbyterian. Next of kin and address: John Alfred Baker, 19 Brighton Road St. Kilda, Victoria. Embarkation Embarked 9.10.16 on ship A65 HMAT Clan MacEwen. Original unit: 11th Light Horse Regiment (Army Pay Corps). Australian War Memorial Embarkation Roll Number: 10/16/3. Later in this publication are more details about the ships that took individuals overseas. Additional Notes File note indicated that he died on 8.8.1952 however it did not disclose where he died nor where buried. Fate 26.12.18 returned to Australia on board the ship Somali from Suez. 16.6.19 Discharged 3rd Military District as medically unfit. Medical condition not disclosed. Believed to have died 8.8.52 but no Victorian death registration located. Recognition Twice Mentioned in Despatches. British War Medal. Victory Medal. Plaque in Western Australia Garden of Remembrance. RSL Virtual War Memorial. Avenue of Honour Tree W4 Additional Notes 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private Baker from Smeaton had been a District Volunteer. Driver from 9.6.16; Corporal from 12.1.17; Acting Staff Sergeant when mentioned in Despatches. Notes 10.10.16 Acting Sergeant Camel Corps while en route by ship to Middle East. 9.11.16 taken on strength with 3rd Light Horse Training Regiment. 31.1.17 transferred to AIF Headquarters in Cairo from 11th Light Horse Regiment. Posted for duty with Australian Army Pay Corps. 13.6.17 while on active service, forfeited 2 days’ pay for failing to salute an officer in Cairo. 3.10.18 admitted to hospital for disease – remained 37 days before discharge. Mentioned in Despatches, Awarded and Promulgated London Gazette No. 31383, dated 5.6.1919, and Commonwealth Gazette No. 113, dated 6.10.1919. 14.11.19 AIF Headquarters Melbourne notified Trooper Baker that he had been Mentioned in Despatches by General Allenby, Commander in Chief, Egyptian Expeditionary Force, “for conspicuous service rendered on behalf of the AIF”. 28.10.20 Having been Mentioned in Despatches twice, he received two Oak Leaves (one large and one small), to be worn with other Military decorations.",
