    "notes": "Prior experience: service with 60th Infantry, Citizen Forces. 20.10.15 first discharged as medically unfit. Driver from 12.3.17; Sapper from 18.7.17. 26.1.18 taken on strength to Equitation School, Engineering Training Unit Moascar, Egypt. 6.3.18 admitted to 2nd Auxiliary Stationary Hospital Moascar to remove tonsils. 20.3.18 discharged to rest Camp Port Said. 10.4.18 discharged back to unit. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private Barnes from Smeaton had been a District Volunteer. Married in 1913 but by first enlistment in September 1914, he declared himself to be single. Place of birth on enlistment papers Richmond, but actually Smeaton. Birth registered at Richmond. Australian National Archives records birth as Melbourne.",
    "militaryMedalCitation": "",
    "fullProfile": "Barnes, Arthur William Family Born Smeaton 1894. Birth Registration 1894/32428. Father: John Barnes. Mother: Elizabeth Agnes Whatmore. Married Florence Marion Twyford 1913. Marriage Registration 1913/2741. Second marriage to Violet Maud Edwards 1919. Marriage Registration 1919/361. Enlistment Originally joined 15.9.14. Second enlistment 20.2.17. Service number: 687 (20306). Rank: Private. Roll title: 14th Infantry Battalion (December 1914). Age on enlistment: 20 years 1 month at first enlistment; 22 years 5 months at second enlistment. Occupation: Railway Employee. Marital status: Single. Religion: Church of England. Address at date of enlistment: “Osborne House” Casterton. Next of kin and address: brother, T. Barnes, Smeaton. Embarkation Embarked Melbourne 22.12.14 on ship A38 HMAT Ulysses. Rate of pay per diem before and after embarkation: Five Shillings. Original unit: 14th Battalion, F Company. Australian War Memorial Embarkation Roll Number: 23/31/1. Fate 10.6.15 (At his first enlistment), returned to Australia on board the ship SS Kyarra. 20.10.15 discharged from 3rd Military District. 28.9.19 (At his second enlistment), returned to Australia on board the ship HT Burma. 30.8.19 discharged 3rd Military District as medically unfit after 410 days of active service. Died 2.7.1966 Canungra Queensland. (Formerly of Creswick.) Age at death, 73 years. Place of burial: Creswick Cemetery. Recognition 1914–15 Star. British War Medal. Victory Medal. RSL Virtual War Memorial. Avenue of Honour Tree E68 Family Military Connection Brother, Private Thomas Barnes [No. 1714], 6th Infantry Batallion, Killed in Action at Gallipoli 7.8.1915. Notes Prior experience: service with 60th Infantry, Citizen Forces. 20.10.15 first discharged as medically unfit. Driver from 12.3.17; Sapper from 18.7.17. 26.1.18 taken on strength to Equitation School, Engineering Training Unit Moascar, Egypt. 6.3.18 admitted to 2nd Auxiliary Stationary Hospital Moascar to remove tonsils. 20.3.18 discharged to rest Camp Port Said. 10.4.18 discharged back to unit. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private Barnes from Smeaton had been a District Volunteer. Married in 1913 but by first enlistment in September 1914, he declared himself to be single. Place of birth on enlistment papers Richmond, but actually Smeaton. Birth registered at Richmond. Australian National Archives records birth as Melbourne.",
