    "id": 27,
    "treeNum": "W39",
    "name": "Beasley, Sister Ethel May Beatrice",
    "gender": "Female",
    "rank": "Staff Nurse",
    "role": "Nurse",
    "location": "Creswick / Newlyn",
    "service": "Joined 17.6.1915. Rank: Staff Nurse. Roll title: No. 1 Australian General Hospital, 1st to 6th and Special Reinforcements, (February 1915 to April 1916). Age on enlistment: 40 years. Occupation: Nurse. Marital status: Single. Religion: Methodist. Next of kin and address: mother, Mrs. Susan Wilkins, 5 Gordon Crescent, Kensington. Embarked Melbourne 17.6.15 on ship A62 HMAT Wandilla. Original unit: No. 1 Australian General Hospital. Rate of pay per diem after embarkation: 7 Shillings and 10 Pence.",
    "family": "Born Newlyn 1875. Birth Registration 1875/8303. Father: Frank Wilkins. Mother: Susan Hooper. Married Frederick James Beasley 6.11.17.",
    "enlistment": "Joined 17.6.1915. Rank: Staff Nurse. Roll title: No. 1 Australian General Hospital, 1st to 6th and Special Reinforcements, (February 1915 to April 1916). Age on enlistment: 40 years. Occupation: Nurse. Marital status: Single. Religion: Methodist. Next of kin and address: mother, Mrs. Susan Wilkins, 5 Gordon Crescent, Kensington.",
    "embarkation": "Embarked Melbourne 17.6.15 on ship A62 HMAT Wandilla. Original unit: No. 1 Australian General Hospital. Rate of pay per diem after embarkation: 7 Shillings and 10 Pence.",
    "fate": "1.1.20 returned to Australia on board HT Aeneas. 6.11.17 resigned commission with AIF upon marriage in London. Died 20.1.48 Richmond. Age at death, 72 years. Death Registration 1948/988. Place of burial: Springvale Botanical Cemetery on 22.1.48 where her cremated ashes were laid to rest.",
    "recognition": "1914–15 Star. British War Medal. Victory Medal. Newlyn State School Honour Board. Newlyn Methodist Church Honour Board. RSL Virtual War Memorial. Avenue of Honour Tree W39",
    "commemoration": "",
    "warService": "",
    "nursingService": "France; England",
    "familyConnection": "",
    "additionalNotes": "Her husband Frederick James Beasley is also represented in the Avenue. They met when he was admitted to her workplace in England.",
    "notes": "Sister Beasley was born Ethel May Beatrice Wilkins but changed her surname upon marriage in England in 1917. Prior experience: trained 3 years at Royal Melbourne and Fairfield Hospitals, 4 years at Bethesda Private Hospital. 3 years as Matron pf Port Moresby Hospital in Papua New Guinea. 20.5.15 applied for Australian Army Nursing Service. 27.5.16 attached for duty to 1st Australian Casualty Clearing Station from 26th General Hospital Rouen, France. 15.9.17 nurse Wilkins made an application to Matron-in- Chief AANS, AIF, for permission to marry. 6.11.17 resigned her appointment in England owing to marriage – only unmarried Australian nurses were permitted to work. 14.11.17 cablegram from Administrative AIF Headquarters London to department of Defence indicating that Nurse Wilkins of 1st General Hospital had resigned her commission on account of her marriage. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Staff Nurse Wilkins from Newlyn had been a District Volunteer.",
    "militaryMedalCitation": "",
    "fullProfile": "Beasley, Sister Ethel May Beatrice Family Born Newlyn 1875. Birth Registration 1875/8303. Father: Frank Wilkins. Mother: Susan Hooper. Married Frederick James Beasley 6.11.17. Enlistment Joined 17.6.1915. Rank: Staff Nurse. Roll title: No. 1 Australian General Hospital, 1st to 6th and Special Reinforcements, (February 1915 to April 1916). Age on enlistment: 40 years. Occupation: Nurse. Marital status: Single. Religion: Methodist. Next of kin and address: mother, Mrs. Susan Wilkins, 5 Gordon Crescent, Kensington. Embarkation Embarked Melbourne 17.6.15 on ship A62 HMAT Wandilla. Original unit: No. 1 Australian General Hospital. Rate of pay per diem after embarkation: 7 Shillings and 10 Pence. Additional Notes Given that the British Government had no restrictions on married women working as nurses, after marrying she applied as nursing staff at Queen’s Hospital, Frognal Avenue, Sidcup, England, where she worked with internationally renowned facial reconstruction surgeon Harold Gillies. Fate 1.1.20 returned to Australia on board HT Aeneas. 6.11.17 resigned commission with AIF upon marriage in London. Died 20.1.48 Richmond. Age at death, 72 years. Death Registration 1948/988. Place of burial: Springvale Botanical Cemetery on 22.1.48 where her cremated ashes were laid to rest. Recognition 1914–15 Star. British War Medal. Victory Medal. Newlyn State School Honour Board. Newlyn Methodist Church Honour Board. RSL Virtual War Memorial. Avenue of Honour Tree W39 Additional Notes Her husband Frederick James Beasley is also represented in the Avenue. They met when he was admitted to her workplace in England. Nursing Service France; England Notes Sister Beasley was born Ethel May Beatrice Wilkins but changed her surname upon marriage in England in 1917. Prior experience: trained 3 years at Royal Melbourne and Fairfield Hospitals, 4 years at Bethesda Private Hospital. 3 years as Matron pf Port Moresby Hospital in Papua New Guinea. 20.5.15 applied for Australian Army Nursing Service. 27.5.16 attached for duty to 1st Australian Casualty Clearing Station from 26th General Hospital Rouen, France. 15.9.17 nurse Wilkins made an application to Matron-in- Chief AANS, AIF, for permission to marry. 6.11.17 resigned her appointment in England owing to marriage – only unmarried Australian nurses were permitted to work. 14.11.17 cablegram from Administrative AIF Headquarters London to department of Defence indicating that Nurse Wilkins of 1st General Hospital had resigned her commission on account of her marriage. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Staff Nurse Wilkins from Newlyn had been a District Volunteer.",
