    "id": 30,
    "treeNum": "W5",
    "name": "Betts, Ernest Alfred",
    "gender": "Male",
    "rank": "Gunner",
    "role": "Soldier",
    "location": "Kingston / Creswick",
    "service": "Joined 11.8.15 Melbourne. Service number: 9291. Rank: Gunner. Roll title: 4th Field Artillery Brigade, 1st to 5th Reinforcements, (November 1915 to May 1916). Age on enlistment: 20 years 9 months. Marital status: single. Religion: Presbyterian. Occupation: Clerk. Address at date of enlistment: 16 Railway Place Newmarket Melbourne. Next of kin and address: father, B. Betts, Mining Manager, Rokewood. Embarked Melbourne 18.11.15 on ship A18 HMAT Wiltshire. Rate of pay per diem before and after embarkation: Five Shillings. Original unit: Field Artillery 4th Brigade, 1st Reinforcement. Australian War Memorial Embarkation Roll Number: 13/32/2. Later in this publication are more details about the ships that took individuals overseas.",
    "family": "Born Allendale 1894. Birth Registration 1894/25774. Father: Benjamin Betts. Mother: Julia Anna Oates. Married Bertha Elizabeth Calman 1921. Marriage Registration 1921/8599.",
    "enlistment": "Joined 11.8.15 Melbourne. Service number: 9291. Rank: Gunner. Roll title: 4th Field Artillery Brigade, 1st to 5th Reinforcements, (November 1915 to May 1916). Age on enlistment: 20 years 9 months. Marital status: single. Religion: Presbyterian. Occupation: Clerk. Address at date of enlistment: 16 Railway Place Newmarket Melbourne. Next of kin and address: father, B. Betts, Mining Manager, Rokewood.",
    "embarkation": "Embarked Melbourne 18.11.15 on ship A18 HMAT Wiltshire. Rate of pay per diem before and after embarkation: Five Shillings. Original unit: Field Artillery 4th Brigade, 1st Reinforcement. Australian War Memorial Embarkation Roll Number: 13/32/2. Later in this publication are more details about the ships that took individuals overseas.",
    "fate": "8.4.18 returned to Australia on board the ship HT Dunluce Castle. 26.9.18 discharged 3rd Military District as medically unfit with neurasthenia. Died 21.3.1975 MacLeod Repatriation Hospital. Age at death, 80 years. Death Registration 1975/7583.",
    "recognition": "1914–15 Star. British War Medal. Victory Medal. Plaque in Victorian Garden of Remembrance. Kingston Fire Brigade Honour Board. Kingston State School Honour Board. RSL Virtual War Memorial. Avenue of Honour Tree W5",
    "commemoration": "",
    "warService": "",
    "nursingService": "",
    "familyConnection": "",
    "additionalNotes": "Was confined for many months in a south England hospital, where he was taught embroidery as part of his rehabilitation. Also recorded as returning to Australia on the Port Darwin 12.7.18.",
    "notes": "15.12.15 disembarked Suez and immediately attached to 4th Field Artillery Brigade Heliopolis, Egypt. 5.2.16 posted to 11th Battery Tel-el-Kebir, Egypt. 14.3.16 embarked Alexandria, Egypt to join the BEF [British Expeditionary Force] in France. 19.3.16 disembarked Marseilles. 12.4.17 Wounded in Action. (Shell concussion to spine.) 15.4.17 treated by 6th Australian Field Ambulance before being transferred to 45 Casualty Clearing Station. 24.4.17 was evacuated from Rouen on hospital ship Aberdonian for London. 26.4.17 admitted to Military Hospital Lewisham. 15.5.17 discharged for duty. 10.7.17 readmitted to 3rd London General Hospital Wandsworth. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Gunner Betts from Kingston had been a District Volunteer. 31.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported on all from the Borough and Shire who had been Wounded in Action during WW1 and that included Private Betts. At the time of his death he was recorded as being a resident of Balwyn and his occupation retired.",
    "militaryMedalCitation": "",
    "fullProfile": "Betts, Ernest Alfred Family Born Allendale 1894. Birth Registration 1894/25774. Father: Benjamin Betts. Mother: Julia Anna Oates. Married Bertha Elizabeth Calman 1921. Marriage Registration 1921/8599. Enlistment Joined 11.8.15 Melbourne. Service number: 9291. Rank: Gunner. Roll title: 4th Field Artillery Brigade, 1st to 5th Reinforcements, (November 1915 to May 1916). Age on enlistment: 20 years 9 months. Marital status: single. Religion: Presbyterian. Occupation: Clerk. Address at date of enlistment: 16 Railway Place Newmarket Melbourne. Next of kin and address: father, B. Betts, Mining Manager, Rokewood. Embarkation Embarked Melbourne 18.11.15 on ship A18 HMAT Wiltshire. Rate of pay per diem before and after embarkation: Five Shillings. Original unit: Field Artillery 4th Brigade, 1st Reinforcement. Australian War Memorial Embarkation Roll Number: 13/32/2. Later in this publication are more details about the ships that took individuals overseas. Fate 8.4.18 returned to Australia on board the ship HT Dunluce Castle. 26.9.18 discharged 3rd Military District as medically unfit with neurasthenia. Died 21.3.1975 MacLeod Repatriation Hospital. Age at death, 80 years. Death Registration 1975/7583. Recognition 1914–15 Star. British War Medal. Victory Medal. Plaque in Victorian Garden of Remembrance. Kingston Fire Brigade Honour Board. Kingston State School Honour Board. RSL Virtual War Memorial. Avenue of Honour Tree W5 Additional Notes Was confined for many months in a south England hospital, where he was taught embroidery as part of his rehabilitation. Also recorded as returning to Australia on the Port Darwin 12.7.18. Notes 15.12.15 disembarked Suez and immediately attached to 4th Field Artillery Brigade Heliopolis, Egypt. 5.2.16 posted to 11th Battery Tel-el-Kebir, Egypt. 14.3.16 embarked Alexandria, Egypt to join the BEF [British Expeditionary Force] in France. 19.3.16 disembarked Marseilles. 12.4.17 Wounded in Action. (Shell concussion to spine.) 15.4.17 treated by 6th Australian Field Ambulance before being transferred to 45 Casualty Clearing Station. 24.4.17 was evacuated from Rouen on hospital ship Aberdonian for London. 26.4.17 admitted to Military Hospital Lewisham. 15.5.17 discharged for duty. 10.7.17 readmitted to 3rd London General Hospital Wandsworth. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Gunner Betts from Kingston had been a District Volunteer. 31.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported on all from the Borough and Shire who had been Wounded in Action during WW1 and that included Private Betts. At the time of his death he was recorded as being a resident of Balwyn and his occupation retired.",
