    "id": 37,
    "treeNum": "W7",
    "name": "Bourke, John Thomas",
    "gender": "Male",
    "rank": "Sapper",
    "role": "Soldier",
    "location": "Creswick / Allendale",
    "service": "Joined 8.10.15 Melbourne. Service number: 7731. Rank: Sapper. Roll title: No 1 Pack Wireless Signal Troop (February 1916). Age on enlistment: 23 years. Occupation: Telegraph operator (Melbourne). Marital status: Single. Religion: Roman Catholic. Next of kin and address: father, John Bourke, Branxholme, Victoria. Embarked Melbourne 5.2.16 on ship A12 HMAT Saldanha. Rate of pay per diem before and after embarkation: Five Shillings. Original unit: Wireless Signal Troop Pack 1. Australian War Memorial Embarkation Roll Number: 22/32/1.",
    "family": "Born Murtoa 1892. Birth Registration 1892/35017. Father: John Bourke. Mother: Adeline Mary Smith.",
    "enlistment": "Joined 8.10.15 Melbourne. Service number: 7731. Rank: Sapper. Roll title: No 1 Pack Wireless Signal Troop (February 1916). Age on enlistment: 23 years. Occupation: Telegraph operator (Melbourne). Marital status: Single. Religion: Roman Catholic. Next of kin and address: father, John Bourke, Branxholme, Victoria.",
    "embarkation": "Embarked Melbourne 5.2.16 on ship A12 HMAT Saldanha. Rate of pay per diem before and after embarkation: Five Shillings. Original unit: Wireless Signal Troop Pack 1. Australian War Memorial Embarkation Roll Number: 22/32/1.",
    "fate": "22.3.19 returned to Australia on board the ship Janus from Bombay. 3.6.19 discharged 3rd Military District. Died 25.6.1949 Hamilton. Age at death, 56 years. Death Registration 1949/20904.",
    "recognition": "Meritorious Service Medal. Mentioned in Despatches. British War Medal. Victory Medal. Smeaton State School Honour Board. RSL Virtual War Memorial. Avenue of Honour Tree W7",
    "commemoration": "",
    "warService": "",
    "nursingService": "",
    "familyConnection": "",
    "additionalNotes": "Also served in World War 2, Service Number: V351284, Warrant Officer Class II. Discharged 18.9.1944. Meritorious Service Medal Citation: “To non-commissioned officer …in recognition of valuable services rendered with British Forces in Mesopotamia…”",
    "notes": "5.2.16 as ship’s wireless operator, he sailed on the Saldanha from Melbourne to Colombo. 25.2.16 as ship’s wireless operator, sailed on the Sardinia from Colombo, Ceylon (now Sri Lanka) to Bombay, India. 10.3.16 as ship’s wireless operator, he sailed on the Teesta from Bombay to Basra (in the Middle East). 9.6.16 absorbed into the 1st Australian and New Zealand Signal Squadron. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private Bourke from Allendale had been a District Volunteer. 23.9.20 was awarded an Oak Leaf to be worn with other Military Decorations. Australian National Archives and enlistment papers record place of birth as Murtoa. While serving with 1st Australian Wireless Signal Squadron, Sapper Bourke was Mentioned in Despatches. Awarded and promulgated in London Gazette Fifth Supplement, No. 30570, dated 12.3.1918, by Lieutenant General Sir Stanley Maud KGB, Commander-in-Chief Mesopotamian Expeditionary Force. See also Commonwealth Gazette No. 16, dated 12.2.1920.",
    "militaryMedalCitation": "",
    "fullProfile": "Bourke, John Thomas Family Born Murtoa 1892. Birth Registration 1892/35017. Father: John Bourke. Mother: Adeline Mary Smith. Enlistment Joined 8.10.15 Melbourne. Service number: 7731. Rank: Sapper. Roll title: No 1 Pack Wireless Signal Troop (February 1916). Age on enlistment: 23 years. Occupation: Telegraph operator (Melbourne). Marital status: Single. Religion: Roman Catholic. Next of kin and address: father, John Bourke, Branxholme, Victoria. Embarkation Embarked Melbourne 5.2.16 on ship A12 HMAT Saldanha. Rate of pay per diem before and after embarkation: Five Shillings. Original unit: Wireless Signal Troop Pack 1. Australian War Memorial Embarkation Roll Number: 22/32/1. Additional Notes Often known as Thomas. On death registration, mother’s name recorded as Mary Adeline Smith. Mother’s family lived in Smeaton. At the time of his death, he was a resident of Hamilton and his occupation was clerk. Fate 22.3.19 returned to Australia on board the ship Janus from Bombay. 3.6.19 discharged 3rd Military District. Died 25.6.1949 Hamilton. Age at death, 56 years. Death Registration 1949/20904. Recognition Meritorious Service Medal. Mentioned in Despatches. British War Medal. Victory Medal. Smeaton State School Honour Board. RSL Virtual War Memorial. Avenue of Honour Tree W7 Additional Notes Also served in World War 2, Service Number: V351284, Warrant Officer Class II. Discharged 18.9.1944. Meritorious Service Medal Citation: “To non-commissioned officer …in recognition of valuable services rendered with British Forces in Mesopotamia…” Notes 5.2.16 as ship’s wireless operator, he sailed on the Saldanha from Melbourne to Colombo. 25.2.16 as ship’s wireless operator, sailed on the Sardinia from Colombo, Ceylon (now Sri Lanka) to Bombay, India. 10.3.16 as ship’s wireless operator, he sailed on the Teesta from Bombay to Basra (in the Middle East). 9.6.16 absorbed into the 1st Australian and New Zealand Signal Squadron. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private Bourke from Allendale had been a District Volunteer. 23.9.20 was awarded an Oak Leaf to be worn with other Military Decorations. Australian National Archives and enlistment papers record place of birth as Murtoa. While serving with 1st Australian Wireless Signal Squadron, Sapper Bourke was Mentioned in Despatches. Awarded and promulgated in London Gazette Fifth Supplement, No. 30570, dated 12.3.1918, by Lieutenant General Sir Stanley Maud KGB, Commander-in-Chief Mesopotamian Expeditionary Force. See also Commonwealth Gazette No. 16, dated 12.2.1920.",
