    "notes": "Prior experience: two years with Senior Cadets and six months with Citizen Forces. 22.4.17 attended Signals School Broadmeadows. 17.5.17 letter signed by both his father and mother to Officer In Charge at Signals School, consented to their son embarking on active service. 23.10.17 taken on strength with 1st Signal Squadron Engineers in Alexandria, Egypt. 2.6.18 treated at 2nd Australian Stationary Hospital Moascar, Egypt for illness. 4.7.18 returned to Camp from Hospital. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Sapper Cassell from Rocky Lead had been a District Volunteer. 19.4.19 forfeited 2 days’ pay for being absent from camp without permission. Penalty imposed by Commanding Officer 4th Light Horse Regiment. 19.10.17 to 15.6.19 was on active service in Egypt. Also served in World War 2. Service number: V401091. Enlisted 31.10.1943, discharged 8.7.1945. Private, 9th Battalion Volunteer Defence Corps.",
    "militaryMedalCitation": "",
    "fullProfile": "Cassell, Ernest Family Born 3.11.1898 Rocklyn. Birth Registration 1899/5794. Father: Conrad Cassell. Mother: Clara Adams. Married Lucy Eveline Giles 1925. Marriage Registration 1925/3317. Enlistment Joined 13.3.17 Melbourne. Service number: 19219. Rank: Sapper. Roll title: 1st to 5th Divisional Signal Companies. Age on enlistment: 18 years 4 months. Occupation: Clerk (Melbourne). Marital status: Single. Religion: Church of England. Next of kin and address: father, Conrad Cassell, Charlton. Embarkation Embarked Sydney 3.9.17 on the ship A55 HMAT Kyarra. Rate of pay per diem before and after embarkation: Five Shillings. Original unit: Special Draft reinforcements, Signal Service (Egypt). Later in this publication are more details about the ships that took individuals overseas. Additional Notes Father Conrad Cassell was Permanent Way Man at Rocky Lead for 11 years. Moved to Charlton in 1914 to be a ganger on the Sea Lake Line, while Clara was Station Mistress and Post Office Operator. Fate 15.6.19 returned to Australia on board the ship HT Essex from Suez. 24.8.19 discharged 3rd Military District. Died 1959 McKinnon. Age at death, 60 years. Death Registration 1959/3246. Cremated at Springvale Botanical Cemetery 3.3.1959 and ashes taken. Recognition Victory Medal. British War Medal. Creswick State School Honour Board. RSL Virtual War Memorial. Avenue of Honour Tree E117 Additional Notes On death registration, mother’s maiden name spelt Amadie. War Service consisted of Middle East only. Family Military Connection Brother, Private George Henry Cassell, [No. 753A], 1st Machine Gun Company, returned to Australia 5.4.19. Notes Prior experience: two years with Senior Cadets and six months with Citizen Forces. 22.4.17 attended Signals School Broadmeadows. 17.5.17 letter signed by both his father and mother to Officer In Charge at Signals School, consented to their son embarking on active service. 23.10.17 taken on strength with 1st Signal Squadron Engineers in Alexandria, Egypt. 2.6.18 treated at 2nd Australian Stationary Hospital Moascar, Egypt for illness. 4.7.18 returned to Camp from Hospital. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Sapper Cassell from Rocky Lead had been a District Volunteer. 19.4.19 forfeited 2 days’ pay for being absent from camp without permission. Penalty imposed by Commanding Officer 4th Light Horse Regiment. 19.10.17 to 15.6.19 was on active service in Egypt. Also served in World War 2. Service number: V401091. Enlisted 31.10.1943, discharged 8.7.1945. Private, 9th Battalion Volunteer Defence Corps.",
