    "notes": "Prior experience: three years with 71st Battalion Ballarat. 27.12.17 disembarked at Devonport, England. Joined 6th Training Battalion Fovant. 3.1.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Sapper Cassell from Rocky Lead was a District Volunteer. 23.7.18 Wounded in Action. (Gassed.) Treated by 41st Stationary Hospital, France. 3.10.18 discharged. Returned to 21st Battalion the following week. 28.11.18 admitted to 28th Australian Field Ambulance with PUO (pyrexia of unknown origin). 29.11.18 admitted to 41st Casualty Clearing Station. 30.11.18 admitted to 6th General Hospital Rouen, France. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private Cassell from Rocky Lead had been a District Volunteer. 25.1.19 transferred from France to London suffering from influenza. 28.1.19 admitted to Lewisham Military Hospital. 10.2.19 discharged and granted leave until 24.2.19, extended to 3.3.19.",
    "militaryMedalCitation": "",
    "fullProfile": "Cassell, George Henry Family Born Dean 1897. Birth Registration 1897/22393. Father: Conrad Cassell. Mother: Clara Adams. Enlistment Joined 10.6.17 Sea Lake. Service number: 753A. Rank: Private. Roll title: 1st Machine Gun Company, 11th to 14th Reinforcements, (May to October 1917). Age on enlistment: 19 years 9 months. Occupation: Apprentice Compositor (Printer). Marital status: Single. Religion: Church of England. Next of kin and address: father, Conrad Cassell, Charlton. Embarkation Embarked Melbourne 30.10.17 on ship A60 HMAT Aeneas. Rate of pay per diem before and after embarkation: Five Shillings. Original unit: 1st Machine Gun Company, 14th Reinforcement, 24th Battalion. Additional Notes His father Conrad Cassell was Permanent Wayman on the railroad at Rocky Lead for 11 years. Moved to Charlton in 1914 to be a ganger on the Sea Lake Line, while Clara was Station Mistress and Post Office Operator. Fate 5.4.19 returned to Australia aboard Armagh from Devonport. 10.6.19 discharged 3rd Military District. Died 1970 Heidelberg. Age at death, 73 years. Death Registration 1970/20701. Cremated at Altona Memorial Park on 1.9.1970. Recognition British War Medal. Victory Medal. Creswick State School Honour Board. RSL Virtual War Memorial. Avenue of Honour Tree E116 Family Military Connection Brother, Sapper Ernest Cassell [No. 12919], 1st Divisional Signal Company, returned to Australia 15.6.19. Later in this publication are more details about the ships that took individuals overseas. Notes Prior experience: three years with 71st Battalion Ballarat. 27.12.17 disembarked at Devonport, England. Joined 6th Training Battalion Fovant. 3.1.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Sapper Cassell from Rocky Lead was a District Volunteer. 23.7.18 Wounded in Action. (Gassed.) Treated by 41st Stationary Hospital, France. 3.10.18 discharged. Returned to 21st Battalion the following week. 28.11.18 admitted to 28th Australian Field Ambulance with PUO (pyrexia of unknown origin). 29.11.18 admitted to 41st Casualty Clearing Station. 30.11.18 admitted to 6th General Hospital Rouen, France. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private Cassell from Rocky Lead had been a District Volunteer. 25.1.19 transferred from France to London suffering from influenza. 28.1.19 admitted to Lewisham Military Hospital. 10.2.19 discharged and granted leave until 24.2.19, extended to 3.3.19.",
