    "location": "Creswick / Broomfield",
    "service": "Joined 12.7.15 Melbourne. Service number: 495. Rank: Private. Roll title: 29th Infantry Battalion (November 1915). Age on enlistment: 33 years. Occupation: Miner. Marital status: Widower. Religion: Methodist. Next of kin and address: sister, Mrs. E. Bell, Eton Park, Whittlesea. Embarked Melbourne 10.11.15 on ship A11 HMAT Ascanius. Rate of pay per diem before and after embarkation: Five Shillings. Original unit: 29th Battalion, B Company. Australian War Memorial Embarkation Roll Number: 23/46/1. Later in this publication are more details about the ships that took individuals overseas.",
    "family": "Born Broomfield 1882. Birth Registration 1882/7301. Father: John Coverdale. Mother: Susanne Frances Mortimer. Married Emma Iles 1904. Second marriage in 1920 to Marie Eva Douglas. Marriage Registration 1920/11856.",
    "enlistment": "Joined 12.7.15 Melbourne. Service number: 495. Rank: Private. Roll title: 29th Infantry Battalion (November 1915). Age on enlistment: 33 years. Occupation: Miner. Marital status: Widower. Religion: Methodist. Next of kin and address: sister, Mrs. E. Bell, Eton Park, Whittlesea.",
    "embarkation": "Embarked Melbourne 10.11.15 on ship A11 HMAT Ascanius. Rate of pay per diem before and after embarkation: Five Shillings. Original unit: 29th Battalion, B Company. Australian War Memorial Embarkation Roll Number: 23/46/1. Later in this publication are more details about the ships that took individuals overseas.",
    "fate": "12.12.18 returned to Australia on board the ship Bakara. 31.3.19 discharged 3rd Military District as medically unfit for further duty. Died 13.3.50 Ballarat. Age at death, 67 years. Death Registration 1950/16182. Place of burial: Creswick Cemetery where he was recorded as a resident of North Creswick.",
    "recognition": "Military Medal. 1914–15 Star. British War Medal. Victory Medal. Broomfield State School Honour Board. RSL Virtual War Memorial. Avenue of Honour Tree W46 Military Medal Citation: Awarded Military Medal for Morlancourt on night of 28th /29th July 1918, for “Conspicuous bravery and devotion to duty”. Full name John Albert Victor Lloyd Coverdale.",
    "commemoration": "",
    "warService": "",
    "nursingService": "",
    "familyConnection": "",
    "additionalNotes": "At the time of his death, he was a resident of Bacchus Marsh and occupation water bailiff. His first wife died in childbirth in 1913.",
    "notes": "23.6.16 disembarked at Marseilles. 22.11.16 treated by 14th Field Ambulance then referred to 38th Casualty Clearing Station, France with PUO (pyrexia of unknown origin). Following assessment by 1st General Hospital in Rouen, casualty invalided to England. 5.12.16 admitted to 2nd War Hospital, Birmingham with PUO. 15.2.17 departed Folkestone for France from Infantry Draft Depot. 1.4.17 Wounded in Action France. (Shotgun wound to face.) Treated by Casualty Clearing Station before being allowed to return to unit on 12.4.17. 2.10.18 admitted to Cheltenham Hospital with influenza. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private Coverdale from Broomfield had been a District Volunteer. 31.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported on all from the Borough and Shire who had been Wounded in Action during WW1 and that included Private Coverdale.",
    "militaryMedalCitation": "",
    "fullProfile": "Coverdale, John Albert Victor Lloyd Family Born Broomfield 1882. Birth Registration 1882/7301. Father: John Coverdale. Mother: Susanne Frances Mortimer. Married Emma Iles 1904. Second marriage in 1920 to Marie Eva Douglas. Marriage Registration 1920/11856. Enlistment Joined 12.7.15 Melbourne. Service number: 495. Rank: Private. Roll title: 29th Infantry Battalion (November 1915). Age on enlistment: 33 years. Occupation: Miner. Marital status: Widower. Religion: Methodist. Next of kin and address: sister, Mrs. E. Bell, Eton Park, Whittlesea. Embarkation Embarked Melbourne 10.11.15 on ship A11 HMAT Ascanius. Rate of pay per diem before and after embarkation: Five Shillings. Original unit: 29th Battalion, B Company. Australian War Memorial Embarkation Roll Number: 23/46/1. Later in this publication are more details about the ships that took individuals overseas. Additional Notes At the time of his death, he was a resident of Bacchus Marsh and occupation water bailiff. His first wife died in childbirth in 1913. Fate 12.12.18 returned to Australia on board the ship Bakara. 31.3.19 discharged 3rd Military District as medically unfit for further duty. Died 13.3.50 Ballarat. Age at death, 67 years. Death Registration 1950/16182. Place of burial: Creswick Cemetery where he was recorded as a resident of North Creswick. Recognition Military Medal. 1914–15 Star. British War Medal. Victory Medal. Broomfield State School Honour Board. RSL Virtual War Memorial. Avenue of Honour Tree W46 Military Medal Citation: Awarded Military Medal for Morlancourt on night of 28th /29th July 1918, for “Conspicuous bravery and devotion to duty”. Full name John Albert Victor Lloyd Coverdale. Notes 23.6.16 disembarked at Marseilles. 22.11.16 treated by 14th Field Ambulance then referred to 38th Casualty Clearing Station, France with PUO (pyrexia of unknown origin). Following assessment by 1st General Hospital in Rouen, casualty invalided to England. 5.12.16 admitted to 2nd War Hospital, Birmingham with PUO. 15.2.17 departed Folkestone for France from Infantry Draft Depot. 1.4.17 Wounded in Action France. (Shotgun wound to face.) Treated by Casualty Clearing Station before being allowed to return to unit on 12.4.17. 2.10.18 admitted to Cheltenham Hospital with influenza. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private Coverdale from Broomfield had been a District Volunteer. 31.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported on all from the Borough and Shire who had been Wounded in Action during WW1 and that included Private Coverdale.",
