    "fullProfile": "Curran, Charles Robert Family Born Smeaton 1886. Birth Registration 1886/29673. Father: Michael Curran. Mother: Catherine Guiney. Enlistment Joined 4.9.16 Melbourne. Service number: 6978. Rank: Private. Roll title: 5th Infantry Battalion, 13th to 23rd Reinforcements, (December 1915 to November 1916). Age on enlistment: 30 years. Occupation: Labourer. Address on joining: c/o Smeaton Post Office. Marital status: Single. Religion: Roman Catholic. Next of kin and address: sister, Mrs. Elizabeth Caligari, King Street Leederville Western Australia. Embarkation Embarked Melbourne 23.11.16 on ship A20 HMAT Hororata. Rate of pay per diem before and after embarkation: Five Shillings. Original unit: 5th Battalion, 23rd Reinforcement. Australian War Memorial Embarkation Roll Number: 23/22/3. Later in this publication are more details about the ships that took individuals overseas. Fate 13.7.19 returned to Australia on board the ship Persic. 8.8.19 discharged 3rd Military District. Died 24.5.1964 Ballarat. Age at death, 77 years. Death Registration 1964/9734. Place of burial: Creswick Cemetery on 26.5.1964, where he was recorded as a resident of Ballarat. Recognition British War Medal. Victory Medal. Smeaton State School Honour Board. RSL Virtual War Memorial. Avenue of Honour Tree E26 Family Military Connections Brother, Private William Curran, [No. 26154], 10th Field Artillery Brigade, returned to Australia 9.2.18. Brother, Private Denis Curran, [No. 1963], 39th Infantry Battalion, returned to Australia 12.2.18. His brothers-in-law Antonio and Baptista Caligari are also in the Avenue. Notes 29.1.17 disembarked Plymouth, England and immediately joined the 2nd Training Battalion Durrington Camp. 3.5.17 proceeded overseas to France via Folkestone from 2nd Training Battalion Durrington camp. 5.10.17 while a member of the 5th Battalion, Wounded in Action Belgium. (Gunshot wound to left side of chest.) Treated by 64th Field Ambulance. Transferred to 11th General Hospital. 1.11.17 returned to unit. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private Curran from Smeaton had been a District Volunteer. 31.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported all from the Borough and Shire who had been Wounded in Action during WW1 and that included Private Curran. 23.5.19 returned to England to prepare for repatriation to Australia. At the time of his death, he was recorded as being a resident of Smeaton and occupation pensioner.",
