    "warService": "",
    "nursingService": "",
    "familyConnection": "",
    "additionalNotes": "2.6.19 embarked Port Said on board HT Nilz for England to commence leave and prepare for repatriation to Australia. At the time of his death, he was recorded as being a resident of Colac and occupation retired farmer.",
    "notes": "5.7.15 admitted to No. 1 Stationary Hospital Mudros, Greece, with dysentery. 14.7.15 treated for bronchitis at No. 1 Stationary Hospital, Mudros. 28.2.18 qualified instructor for 34th Hotchkiss Gun Course. 6.3.18 admitted to 24th Stationary Hospital in Kantara, Egypt for appendicitis. Private from 17.9.14; Corporal from 7.8.15; Sergeant from 3.11.15; 2nd Lieutenant, 8th Light Horse, from 24.12.17; Lieutenant from 29.5.18. Distinguished Conduct Medal Citation: “For conspicuous gallantry and devotion to duty. When the enemy first rushed the position he gallantly rushed to the front and gave great assistance in beating off the initial attack. He then went along the line, rendered the most valuable aid in re- organizing the whole position, taking ammunition along the whole line to every place where it was required. His soldierly bearing and fine fighting spirit were most conspicuous.” London Gazette 1.5.1918.",
    "militaryMedalCitation": "",
    "fullProfile": "Currington, Allan Harry Family Born 5.3.1887 Great Barford England. Father: Arthur Belamy Currington. Mother: Emily Buckle. Married Annie Fox 1921. Marriage Registration 1921/9524. Enlistment Joined 17.9.14 Ballarat. Service number: 353. Rank: Private. Roll title: 8th Light Horse Regiment (February 1915). Age on enlistment: 27 years. Occupation: Labourer. Marital status: Single. Religion: Church of England. Address at date of enlistment: Creswick. Next of kin and address: mother, Mrs. Emily Currington, Bedford Road, Great Barford, Sandy Beds, Bedfordshire, England. Embarkation Embarked Melbourne 25.2.15 on ship A16 HMAT Star of Victoria. Rate of pay per diem before and after embarkation: Five Shillings. Original unit: 8th Light Horse Regiment, C Squadron. Australian War Memorial Embarkation Roll Number: 10/13/1. Additional Notes Worked as a share farmer in Dean prior to the war. He applied for land at Warncoort under the soldier settlement scheme. Fate 23.9.19 returned to Australia aboard the HMAT Ascanius. 12.12.19 discharged 3rd Military District. Died 12.11.63 Geelong. Age at death, 76 years. Death Registration 1963/24170. Place of burial: Warncoort (Geelong) Cemetery. Recognition Distinguished Conduct Medal. 1914–15 Star. British War Medal. Victory Medal. RSL Virtual War Memorial. Avenue of Honour Tree E101 Additional Notes 2.6.19 embarked Port Said on board HT Nilz for England to commence leave and prepare for repatriation to Australia. At the time of his death, he was recorded as being a resident of Colac and occupation retired farmer. Notes 5.7.15 admitted to No. 1 Stationary Hospital Mudros, Greece, with dysentery. 14.7.15 treated for bronchitis at No. 1 Stationary Hospital, Mudros. 28.2.18 qualified instructor for 34th Hotchkiss Gun Course. 6.3.18 admitted to 24th Stationary Hospital in Kantara, Egypt for appendicitis. Private from 17.9.14; Corporal from 7.8.15; Sergeant from 3.11.15; 2nd Lieutenant, 8th Light Horse, from 24.12.17; Lieutenant from 29.5.18. Distinguished Conduct Medal Citation: “For conspicuous gallantry and devotion to duty. When the enemy first rushed the position he gallantly rushed to the front and gave great assistance in beating off the initial attack. He then went along the line, rendered the most valuable aid in re- organizing the whole position, taking ammunition along the whole line to every place where it was required. His soldierly bearing and fine fighting spirit were most conspicuous.” London Gazette 1.5.1918.",
