    "fate": "18.1.19 returned to Australia on board the ship HT Ulysses. 10.3.19 discharged 3rd Military District on medical grounds. Died 5.10.1961. Age at death, 66 years. Death Registration 1961/23138. Place of burial: Springvale Botanical Cemetery on 9.10.1961.",
    "recognition": "British War Medal. Victory Medal. Springmount State School Honour Board. RSL Virtual War Memorial. Avenue of Honour Tree E110",
    "commemoration": "",
    "warService": "",
    "nursingService": "",
    "familyConnection": "",
    "additionalNotes": "31.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported on all from the Borough and Shire who had been Wounded in Action during WW1 and that included Private Davey. Was granted a war pension. At the time of his death, he was recorded as being a resident of Murrumbeena and occupation retired clerk.",
    "notes": "2.12.16 at Sierra Leone, transferred from HMAT Port Lincoln to HMAT Borda. 9.1.17 arrived Plymouth. 13.2.17 admitted to Fargo Military Hospital, England with bronchial pneumonia. 27.10.17 admitted to 5th Australian Field Ambulance from Casualty Clearing Station, France for treatment of trench feet. Evacuated to England on hospital ship Princess Elizabeth. 5.11.17 treated for trench feet at Boscombe Military Hospital, Hampshire, England. 15.1.18 Sutton Veny Camp, England forfeited 2 days’ pay for being absent without permission. Punishment imposed by Lieutenant Colonel Mausbridge. 19.3.18 Wounded in Action. (Serious gunshot wound to left leg.) Transferred to England for amputation of lower left leg. Dangerously ill. 3.4.18 embarked for England on hospital ship Brighton. 4.4.18 admitted to Alexander Hospital, Cosham. 2.7.18 Medical Board determined total incapacity. Birth registered at Creswick.",
    "militaryMedalCitation": "",
    "fullProfile": "Davey, Arthur Richard Family Born Springmount 1895. Birth Registration 1895/20003. Father: Louis Davey. Mother: Sarah Jane Potter. Married Violet Mary Turnbull 1924. Marriage Registration 1924/714. Enlistment Joined 17.6.16 Ballarat. Service number: 2302. Rank: Private. Roll title: 39th Infantry Battalion, 1st to 6th Reinforcements, (May to December 1916). Age on enlistment: 21 years. Occupation: Labourer. Marital status: Single. Religion: Church of England. Next of kin and address: father, Louis Davey, c/o Springmount Post Office. Embarkation Embarked Melbourne 20.10.16 on ship A17 HMAT Port Lincoln. Rate of pay per diem before and after embarkation: Five Shillings. Original unit: 39th Battalion, 4th Reinforcement. Australian War Memorial Embarkation Roll Number: 23/56/2. Later in this publication are more details about the ships that took individuals overseas. Additional Notes 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private Davey from Springmount had been a District Volunteer. Fate 18.1.19 returned to Australia on board the ship HT Ulysses. 10.3.19 discharged 3rd Military District on medical grounds. Died 5.10.1961. Age at death, 66 years. Death Registration 1961/23138. Place of burial: Springvale Botanical Cemetery on 9.10.1961. Recognition British War Medal. Victory Medal. Springmount State School Honour Board. RSL Virtual War Memorial. Avenue of Honour Tree E110 Additional Notes 31.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported on all from the Borough and Shire who had been Wounded in Action during WW1 and that included Private Davey. Was granted a war pension. At the time of his death, he was recorded as being a resident of Murrumbeena and occupation retired clerk. Notes 2.12.16 at Sierra Leone, transferred from HMAT Port Lincoln to HMAT Borda. 9.1.17 arrived Plymouth. 13.2.17 admitted to Fargo Military Hospital, England with bronchial pneumonia. 27.10.17 admitted to 5th Australian Field Ambulance from Casualty Clearing Station, France for treatment of trench feet. Evacuated to England on hospital ship Princess Elizabeth. 5.11.17 treated for trench feet at Boscombe Military Hospital, Hampshire, England. 15.1.18 Sutton Veny Camp, England forfeited 2 days’ pay for being absent without permission. Punishment imposed by Lieutenant Colonel Mausbridge. 19.3.18 Wounded in Action. (Serious gunshot wound to left leg.) Transferred to England for amputation of lower left leg. Dangerously ill. 3.4.18 embarked for England on hospital ship Brighton. 4.4.18 admitted to Alexander Hospital, Cosham. 2.7.18 Medical Board determined total incapacity. Birth registered at Creswick.",
