    "service": "Joined 4.4.16. Rank: Lieutenant (Provisional). Unit: 9th Field Artillery Brigade, 33rd Battery. Age on enlistment: 20 years 6 months. Occupation: Soldier. Marital status: Single. Religion: Presbyterian. Address at enlistment: Royal Military College, Duntroon, New South Wales. Next of kin and address: father, T. H. Davies, 107 Seymour Street, Ballarat. Embarked Sydney 11.5.16 on ship A8 HMAT Argyllshire. Original unit: 9th Field Artillery Brigade. Rate of pay per diem before embarkation: 15 Shillings. Rate of pay per diem after embarkation: 14 Shillings 6 pence. Later in this publication are more details about the ships that took individuals overseas.",
    "family": "Born Ballarat 1895. Birth Registration 1895/26331. Father: Thomas Henry Davies. Mother: Elizabeth Gwen Williams.",
    "enlistment": "Joined 4.4.16. Rank: Lieutenant (Provisional). Unit: 9th Field Artillery Brigade, 33rd Battery. Age on enlistment: 20 years 6 months. Occupation: Soldier. Marital status: Single. Religion: Presbyterian. Address at enlistment: Royal Military College, Duntroon, New South Wales. Next of kin and address: father, T. H. Davies, 107 Seymour Street, Ballarat.",
    "embarkation": "Embarked Sydney 11.5.16 on ship A8 HMAT Argyllshire. Original unit: 9th Field Artillery Brigade. Rate of pay per diem before embarkation: 15 Shillings. Rate of pay per diem after embarkation: 14 Shillings 6 pence. Later in this publication are more details about the ships that took individuals overseas.",
    "fate": "31.5.19 returned to Australia on board the ship HT Aeneas. 26.8.19 as an officer his services were dispensed with as he was no longer required to perform these duties. Died 1956 Ballarat. Death Registration 1956/22573. Place of burial: Ballarat (New) Cemetery.",
    "recognition": "Military Cross and Bar. British War Medal. Victory Medal. Kingston State School Honour Board. Avenue of Honour Tree W141",
    "commemoration": "",
    "warService": "",
    "nursingService": "",
    "familyConnection": "",
    "additionalNotes": "30.9.18 Wounded in Action. (Nature of wounding not disclosed.) Remained on duty. 23.10.18 promoted to Major whilst commanding 3rd Divisional Artillery.",
    "notes": "Prior military service with 19th Light Horse and Royal Military College. 15.7.16 arrived Plymouth, England. 35.12.16 embarked Southampton for Le Havre, France. 20.12.17 Military Cross Citation: “For conspicuous gallantry and devotion to duty as forward observing officer. He worked continually in exposed positions under heavy shell fire, by his initiative and skill transmitting valuable information, later he carried out a successful reconnaissance leading and organizing a party to secure portions of enemy guns.” 3.6.19 Bar to Military Cross Citation: “For conspicuous gallantry and devotion to duty east of Clery on 20th August 1918, in close support of the infantry advance. He directed concentration of fire of the guns as they arrived, and maintained the rate of fire until communications were opened with brigade headquarters. His energy and ability throughout ten days fightings largely contributed to the success of the operation.”",
    "militaryMedalCitation": "",
    "fullProfile": "Davies, David Leslie Family Born Ballarat 1895. Birth Registration 1895/26331. Father: Thomas Henry Davies. Mother: Elizabeth Gwen Williams. Enlistment Joined 4.4.16. Rank: Lieutenant (Provisional). Unit: 9th Field Artillery Brigade, 33rd Battery. Age on enlistment: 20 years 6 months. Occupation: Soldier. Marital status: Single. Religion: Presbyterian. Address at enlistment: Royal Military College, Duntroon, New South Wales. Next of kin and address: father, T. H. Davies, 107 Seymour Street, Ballarat. Embarkation Embarked Sydney 11.5.16 on ship A8 HMAT Argyllshire. Original unit: 9th Field Artillery Brigade. Rate of pay per diem before embarkation: 15 Shillings. Rate of pay per diem after embarkation: 14 Shillings 6 pence. Later in this publication are more details about the ships that took individuals overseas. Additional Notes Lieutenant Davies’ father is mentioned in articles about Blampied Sports Days. Fate 31.5.19 returned to Australia on board the ship HT Aeneas. 26.8.19 as an officer his services were dispensed with as he was no longer required to perform these duties. Died 1956 Ballarat. Death Registration 1956/22573. Place of burial: Ballarat (New) Cemetery. Recognition Military Cross and Bar. British War Medal. Victory Medal. Kingston State School Honour Board. Avenue of Honour Tree W141 Additional Notes 30.9.18 Wounded in Action. (Nature of wounding not disclosed.) Remained on duty. 23.10.18 promoted to Major whilst commanding 3rd Divisional Artillery. Notes Prior military service with 19th Light Horse and Royal Military College. 15.7.16 arrived Plymouth, England. 35.12.16 embarked Southampton for Le Havre, France. 20.12.17 Military Cross Citation: “For conspicuous gallantry and devotion to duty as forward observing officer. He worked continually in exposed positions under heavy shell fire, by his initiative and skill transmitting valuable information, later he carried out a successful reconnaissance leading and organizing a party to secure portions of enemy guns.” 3.6.19 Bar to Military Cross Citation: “For conspicuous gallantry and devotion to duty east of Clery on 20th August 1918, in close support of the infantry advance. He directed concentration of fire of the guns as they arrived, and maintained the rate of fire until communications were opened with brigade headquarters. His energy and ability throughout ten days fightings largely contributed to the success of the operation.”",
