    "familyConnection": "Brother, Private Herbert Godfrey, [No. NZ 12/3026], Killed in Action with No Known Grave 7.6.17.",
    "additionalNotes": "Mentioned in Despatches, “for conspicuous services” rendered by Saddler Sergeant Godfrey of the Australian Imperial Force. Awarded and promulgated London Gazette No. 31448, dated 11.7.1919. At the time of his death, he was recorded as being a resident of Brighton and occupation retired.",
    "notes": "Prior experience: three years with 22nd Battalion, Field Artillery Brigade and 50th Battalion, Senior Cadets. 1.10.15 Gunner with 4th Field Artillery Brigade. 1.11.15 Saddler-Sergeant with 6th Field Artillery Brigade. 21.12.15 disembarked Suez, Egypt and proceeded to join MEF [Mediterranean Expeditionary Force]. 16.3.16 Mentioned in Despatches by Sir Douglas Haig in London Gazette No. 31448, dated 8.7.19. 17.3.16 departed Alexandria, Egypt to join BEF [British Expeditionary Force] France. 23.3.16 disembarked Marseilles. 5.2.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Sergeant Godfrey was a District Volunteer. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Sergeant Godfrey from Clementston was a District Volunteer.",
    "militaryMedalCitation": "",
    "fullProfile": "Godfrey, Cecil Edwin Family Born Clementston 1894. Birth Registration 1894/17065. Father: Thomas Alfred Godfrey. Mother: Elizabeth Matthews. Married Emily Maud Croft 1925. Marriage Registration 1925/3220. Enlistment Joined 17.7.15 Melbourne. Service number: 8606. Rank: Gunner. Roll title: 6 FAB [Field Artillery Brigade] (November 1915). Age on enlistment: 21 years. Occupation: Saddler. Marital status: Single. Religion: Methodist. Next of kin and address: father, Thomas Alfred Godfrey, Tuson Street, Ararat. Embarkation Embarked Melbourne 22.11.15 on ship A34 HMAT Persic. Rate of pay per diem before embarkation: 10 Shillings and 6 Pence. Rate of pay per diem after embarkation: 9 Shillings and 6 Pence. Original unit: Field Artillery Brigade 6, Brigade Ammunition Column. Australian War Memorial Embarkation Roll Number: 13/34/1. Later in this publication are more details about the ships that took individuals overseas. Fate 10.6 .19 returned to Australia aboard ship HT Runic. 2.8.19 discharged 3rd Military District. Died 2.6.1975 Heidelberg. Age at death, 80 years. Death Registration 1975/12719. Place of burial: Springvale Botanical Cemetery on 4.6.1975. Recognition Mentioned in Despatches. 1914–15 Star. British War Medal. Victory Medal. RSL Virtual War Memorial. Avenue of Honour Tree E134 Additional Notes Mentioned in Despatches, “for conspicuous services” rendered by Saddler Sergeant Godfrey of the Australian Imperial Force. Awarded and promulgated London Gazette No. 31448, dated 11.7.1919. At the time of his death, he was recorded as being a resident of Brighton and occupation retired. Notes Prior experience: three years with 22nd Battalion, Field Artillery Brigade and 50th Battalion, Senior Cadets. 1.10.15 Gunner with 4th Field Artillery Brigade. 1.11.15 Saddler-Sergeant with 6th Field Artillery Brigade. 21.12.15 disembarked Suez, Egypt and proceeded to join MEF [Mediterranean Expeditionary Force]. 16.3.16 Mentioned in Despatches by Sir Douglas Haig in London Gazette No. 31448, dated 8.7.19. 17.3.16 departed Alexandria, Egypt to join BEF [British Expeditionary Force] France. 23.3.16 disembarked Marseilles. 5.2.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Sergeant Godfrey was a District Volunteer. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Sergeant Godfrey from Clementston was a District Volunteer. Family Military Connection Brother, Private Herbert Godfrey, [No. NZ 12/3026], Killed in Action with No Known Grave 7.6.17.",
