    "additionalNotes": "At the time of his death, he was recorded as being a resident of Castlemaine with an occupation of farmer. Family Military Connections Cousin, Private George Alfred Heron, [No. 4344], 14th Infantry Battalion, Killed in Action France 8.8.18. Cousin, Corporal Walter Francis Heron [No. 1185], 15th Infantry Battalion, returned to Australia 1.11.18.",
    "notes": "7.12.15 disembarked Alexandria, Egypt. 4.2.16 admitted to 2nd Australian General Hospital Ghezireh, Egypt. 7.2.16 admitted to No. 4 Auxiliary Hospital Cairo with mumps. 20.4.16 transferred from 21st Infantry Battalion to 57th Battalion. 29.5.16 transferred to 15th Training Battalion Moascar camp. 17.9.17 admitted to 3rd Canadian General Hospital Boulogne, France with deformed feet (spur on right foot). 7.10.17 Embarked for England for treatment. 18.10.17 admitted to 1st Southern General Hospital, Birmingham with pes cavus (hollow foot). 23.10.17 transferred to 1st Auxiliary Hospital, Harefield. 26.7.18 granted a pension of fifteen shillings per fortnight. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private Heron from Kooroocheang had been a District Volunteer. On his death registration, mother’s surname was recorded as Gervasoni.",
    "militaryMedalCitation": "",
    "fullProfile": "Heron, Duncan Family Born Newstead in 1897. Birth Registration 1897/5435. Father: Duncan Heron. Mother: Annie Allen. Married Mary Schultz 1919. Marriage Registration 1919/3123. Enlistment Joined 20.1.15 Castlemaine. Service number: 392. Rank: Private. Roll title: 21st Infantry Battalion (May 1915). Age on enlistment: 18 years 1 month. Marital status: Single. Religion: Catholic. Address at date of enlistment: Sandon near Newstead. Next of kin and address: grandmother, Mrs. Sarah Heron, near Newstead. Embarkation Embarked Melbourne 10.5.15 on ship A38 HMAT Ulysses. Rate of pay per diem before and after embarkation: Five Shillings. Original unit from Nominal Roll: 57th Battalion. Australian War Memorial Embarkation Roll Number: 23/38/1. Later in this publication are more details about the ships that took individuals overseas. Fate 21.4.18 return to Australia on board the Suevic from Weymouth. 25.76.18 discharged from 3rd Military District as medically unfit with pes cavus. Died 1972 at Castlemaine. Age at death, 95 years. Death Registration 1972/26498. Recognition 1914–15 Star. British War Medal. Victory Medal. RSL Virtual War Memorial. Avenue of Honour Tree E42 Additional Notes At the time of his death, he was recorded as being a resident of Castlemaine with an occupation of farmer. Family Military Connections Cousin, Private George Alfred Heron, [No. 4344], 14th Infantry Battalion, Killed in Action France 8.8.18. Cousin, Corporal Walter Francis Heron [No. 1185], 15th Infantry Battalion, returned to Australia 1.11.18. Notes 7.12.15 disembarked Alexandria, Egypt. 4.2.16 admitted to 2nd Australian General Hospital Ghezireh, Egypt. 7.2.16 admitted to No. 4 Auxiliary Hospital Cairo with mumps. 20.4.16 transferred from 21st Infantry Battalion to 57th Battalion. 29.5.16 transferred to 15th Training Battalion Moascar camp. 17.9.17 admitted to 3rd Canadian General Hospital Boulogne, France with deformed feet (spur on right foot). 7.10.17 Embarked for England for treatment. 18.10.17 admitted to 1st Southern General Hospital, Birmingham with pes cavus (hollow foot). 23.10.17 transferred to 1st Auxiliary Hospital, Harefield. 26.7.18 granted a pension of fifteen shillings per fortnight. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private Heron from Kooroocheang had been a District Volunteer. On his death registration, mother’s surname was recorded as Gervasoni.",
