    "notes": "Prior experience: three years with 30th Australian Army Service Corps. 1.12.15 driver for 1st Australian Reserve Park. 23.2.16 disembarked Zeitoun, Egypt and immediately proceeded to Alexandria. 15.3.16 taken on strength with 15th Company Army Service Corps from 18th Company. 8.4.16 admitted to 15th Field Ambulance Alexandria, Egypt with PUO (pyrexia of unknown origin). 16.6.16 rejoined unit at Alexandria. 15.3.17 taken on strength from 10th Company AASC [Australian Army Service Corps] to 18th AASC. 30.8.17 embarked England for France. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private Hobill from Melbourne had been a District Volunteer. 26.4.19 admitted to 39th General Hospital London with scabies. At the time of his death, he was recorded as being a resident of Newport with an occupation of retired munitions worker.",
    "militaryMedalCitation": "",
    "fullProfile": "Hobill, Alfred Edward Family Born Allendale 1889. Birth Registration 1889/17819. Father: Edward James Hobill. Mother: Catherine Jewell. Married Cecelia Eva Spear 1920. Marriage Registration 1920/14502. Enlistment Joined Melbourne 13.7.15. Service number: 9393. Rank: Driver. Roll title: 1st Australian Reserve Park, 10th Company ASC [Army Service Corps] Reinforcements, (December 1914 to January 1916). Age on enlistment: 25 years 11 months. Occupation: Butcher. Marital status: Single. Religion: Methodist. Next of kin and address: father, Thomas Hobill, 12 Nelson Street Newport. Embarkation Embarked Melbourne 18.1.16 on ship A44 HMAT Vestalia. Rate of pay per diem before and after embarkation: Six Shillings. Original unit: 1st Australian Reserve Park, 13th Reinforcement 18th Company, Australian Army Service Corps. Australian War Memorial Embarkation Roll Number: 25/34/2. Later in this publication are more details about the ships that took individuals overseas. Fate 23.6.19 returned to Australia on board the ship Ovita. 29.11.19 discharged 3rd Military District. Died 24.8.1965 Williamstown. Age at death, 75 years. Death Registration 1965/19324. Place of burial: Methodist Section, Williamstown Cemetery 27.8.65. Recognition British War Medal. Victory Medal. Allendale State School Honour Board. RSL Virtual War Memorial. Avenue of Honour Tree W136 Family Military Connections Brother, Driver Charles Robert Hobill, [No. 672], Divisional Train, Died of Sickness 20.12.14. Brother, Private Henry Thomas Hobill, [No. 11779], 5th Divisional Train, returned to Australia 20.7.19. Cousin, Driver George William Hobill, [No. 9395], 1st Australian Reserve Park, returned to Australia 23.6.19. Cousin, Driver Henry James Hobill, [No. 9394], 1st Australian Reserve Park, returned to Australia 23.6.19. Notes Prior experience: three years with 30th Australian Army Service Corps. 1.12.15 driver for 1st Australian Reserve Park. 23.2.16 disembarked Zeitoun, Egypt and immediately proceeded to Alexandria. 15.3.16 taken on strength with 15th Company Army Service Corps from 18th Company. 8.4.16 admitted to 15th Field Ambulance Alexandria, Egypt with PUO (pyrexia of unknown origin). 16.6.16 rejoined unit at Alexandria. 15.3.17 taken on strength from 10th Company AASC [Australian Army Service Corps] to 18th AASC. 30.8.17 embarked England for France. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private Hobill from Melbourne had been a District Volunteer. 26.4.19 admitted to 39th General Hospital London with scabies. At the time of his death, he was recorded as being a resident of Newport with an occupation of retired munitions worker.",
