    "commemoration": "",
    "warService": "",
    "nursingService": "",
    "familyConnection": "",
    "additionalNotes": "31.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported on all from the Borough and Shire who had been Wounded in Action including Lieutenant Hodgson. Mentioned in Despatches, awarded and promulgated, London Gazette 5.8.15.",
    "notes": "Military Education included 3½ years at Royal Military College Duntroon and held the rank of Lieutenant in Australian Military Force. 1.5.15 Wounded in Action. (Gunshot wound to left thigh.) Admitted to hospital in Gallipoli with left thigh wound later diagnosed as a broken pelvis. Admitted to 17th General Hospital where he was reported as being dangerously ill. 24.9.15 removed from dangerously ill list. 27.6.15 promoted to Captain in Gallipoli. 29.6.15 identified in Army Corps Routine Orders as having performed Conspicuous Acts of Gallantry between 25th April and 1st May. 13.10.16 Creswick Advertiser reported that Lieutenant Hodgson was Wounded in Action. 9.1.18 advised by letter of his Croix de Guerre decoration from the French War Office, in recognition of his distinguished service during the campaign in the Middle East. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Lieutenant Hodgson from Colac had been a District Volunteer.",
    "militaryMedalCitation": "",
    "fullProfile": "Hodgson, William Roy Family Born Kingston 22.5.1892. Birth Registration 1892/14857. Father: Robert Hodgson. Mother: Margaret Wilson. Married Muriel Doris McDowell 1919. Marriage Registration 1919/9086. Enlistment Joined 14.8.14. Rank: Lieutenant. Roll title: 2nd FAB [Field Artillery Brigade], (October 1914). Age on enlistment: 22 years 4 months. Marital status: Single. Occupation: Soldier. Next of kin and address: Mrs. R. Hodgson, Colac. Embarkation Embarked Melbourne 20.10.14 on ship A9 HMAT Shropshire. Rate of pay per diem before embarkation: Fifteen Shillings. Rate of pay per diem after embarkation: Fourteen Shillings Six Pence. Original unit: Field Artillery 2nd Brigade 5th Battery, 1st Division Artillery. Australian War Memorial Embarkation Roll Number: 13/30/1. Additional Notes William Hodgson also received the following decorations for service: OBE [Order of the British Empire], and CMG [Companion of the Order of St. Michael and St. George]. Fate 22.2.17 returned to Australia on board the ship Karola. 2.7.17 discharged 3rd Military District as medically unfit. Died 1978. Age at death, 86 years. Cremated at Altona Memorial Park. Recognition Croix de Guerre (France). 1914–15 Star. British War Medal. Victory Medal. Mentioned in Despatches. Kingston State School Honour Board. RSL Virtual War Memorial. Avenue of Honour Tree E143 Additional Notes 31.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported on all from the Borough and Shire who had been Wounded in Action including Lieutenant Hodgson. Mentioned in Despatches, awarded and promulgated, London Gazette 5.8.15. Notes Military Education included 3½ years at Royal Military College Duntroon and held the rank of Lieutenant in Australian Military Force. 1.5.15 Wounded in Action. (Gunshot wound to left thigh.) Admitted to hospital in Gallipoli with left thigh wound later diagnosed as a broken pelvis. Admitted to 17th General Hospital where he was reported as being dangerously ill. 24.9.15 removed from dangerously ill list. 27.6.15 promoted to Captain in Gallipoli. 29.6.15 identified in Army Corps Routine Orders as having performed Conspicuous Acts of Gallantry between 25th April and 1st May. 13.10.16 Creswick Advertiser reported that Lieutenant Hodgson was Wounded in Action. 9.1.18 advised by letter of his Croix de Guerre decoration from the French War Office, in recognition of his distinguished service during the campaign in the Middle East. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Lieutenant Hodgson from Colac had been a District Volunteer.",
