    "familyConnection": "",
    "additionalNotes": "At the time of his death, he was recorded as being a resident of Creswick with an occupation of wood carter. Mother’s surname recorded as unknown on death registration.",
    "notes": "Prior experience: six to seven months with Mounted Rifles in New Zealand ten years earlier. 31.5.15 Wounded in Action at the Dardanelles. (Gunshot wound and fracture to foot.) 7.6.15 admitted to 1st General Hospital Heliopolis, Egypt. 15.7.15 discharged for duty in Cairo. 2.8.16 embarked at Alexandria. 24.1.17 detached for duty with Headquarters AIF [Australian Imperial Force] Depot in UK. 15.5.17 Acting Sergeant; 19.10.17 Sergeant. 12.5.18 discharged as medically unfit for duty with chronic bronchitis. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private Hopkins from Allendale had been a District Volunteer. 31.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported on all from the Borough and Shire who had been Wounded in Action during WW1 and that included Sergeant Hopkins. Birth registration shows birth was registered at Maryborough.",
    "militaryMedalCitation": "",
    "fullProfile": "Hopkins, Lewis Llewellyn Family Born Maryborough 1874. Birth Registration 1874/10061. Father: John Hopkins. Mother: Elizabeth Coates. Married Harriette Byers Robinson 1900. Marriage Registration 1900/6489. Enlistment Joined 15.9.14 Kingston. Service number: 208. Rank: Private. Roll title: 14th Infantry Battalion (December 1914). Age on enlistment: 37 years. Occupation: Labourer. Marital status: Married. Religion: Church of England. Next of kin and address: wife, Mrs. L. L. Hopkins, Allendale. Embarkation Embarked Melbourne 22.12.14 on ship A38 HMAT Ulysses. Rate of pay per diem before and after embarkation: Five Shillings. Original unit: 14th Battalion, B Company. Australian War Memorial Embarkation Roll Number: 23/31/1. Later in this publication are more details about the ships that took individuals overseas. Fate 12.5.18 returned to Australia on board the ship Gaika. 22.7.18 discharged 3rd Military District as medically unfit. Died 7.6.1943 Creswick. Age at death, 69 years. Death Registration 1943/17452. Place of burial: Creswick Cemetery on 8.6.1943, where he was recorded as a resident of Creswick. Recognition 1914–15 Star. British War Medal. Victory Medal. RSL Virtual War Memorial. Avenue of Honour Tree W73 Additional Notes At the time of his death, he was recorded as being a resident of Creswick with an occupation of wood carter. Mother’s surname recorded as unknown on death registration. Notes Prior experience: six to seven months with Mounted Rifles in New Zealand ten years earlier. 31.5.15 Wounded in Action at the Dardanelles. (Gunshot wound and fracture to foot.) 7.6.15 admitted to 1st General Hospital Heliopolis, Egypt. 15.7.15 discharged for duty in Cairo. 2.8.16 embarked at Alexandria. 24.1.17 detached for duty with Headquarters AIF [Australian Imperial Force] Depot in UK. 15.5.17 Acting Sergeant; 19.10.17 Sergeant. 12.5.18 discharged as medically unfit for duty with chronic bronchitis. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private Hopkins from Allendale had been a District Volunteer. 31.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported on all from the Borough and Shire who had been Wounded in Action during WW1 and that included Sergeant Hopkins. Birth registration shows birth was registered at Maryborough.",
