    "additionalNotes": "Worked as an accountant with the Railway Department in Melbourne.",
    "notes": "11.4.16 promoted to Corporal, with temporary promotion to Quartermaster Sergeant for duration of voyage to England. 19.6.16 disembarked Plymouth, England and immediately joined 2nd Training Battalion at Durrington Camp. Reverted to rank of Corporal from Quartermaster Sergeant. 19.6.17 proceeded overseas to France via Southampton from 2nd Training Battalion. 8.11.17 In his will, he left all of his possessions and property to his father. 27.6.18 taken on strength of 8th Battalion from 15th Reinforcements. 23.1.18 Army Report concluded that soldier had been Killed in Action in the field of combat in Belgium. 31.12.18 Creswick Advertiser published a list of all from the Borough and Shire who had been Killed in Action including Corporal Johannsen. Family Military Connections Brother, Henry Neiman Johannsen, [No. 6946], 8th Infantry Battalion, returned to Australia 4.9.17. Cousin, Rupert Claude Johannsen, [No. 279], 29th Infantry Battalion, returned to Australia 13.4.19.",
    "militaryMedalCitation": "",
    "fullProfile": "Johannsen, Peter Norman Family Born Allendale in 1889. Birth Registration 1889/17821. Father: John (Jack) Johannsen. Mother: Mary Anne Theresa Keehan. Enlistment Joined 13.7.15 Melbourne. Service number: 4807. Original Rank: Private. Roll title: 8th Infantry Battalion, 13th to 23rd Reinforcements, (December 1915 to November 1916). Age on enlistment: 25 years 10 months. Occupation: Railway Clerk. Marital status: Single. Religion: Church of England. Next of kin and address: father, Mr. Jack Johannsen, Allendale. Embarkation Embarked 7.3.16 Melbourne on ship A18 HMAT Wiltshire. Rate of pay per diem before and after embarkation: Five Shillings. Original unit: 8th Infantry battalion. Australian War Memorial Embarkation Roll Number: 23/25/5. Additional Notes 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private Johannsen from Allendale had been a District Volunteer. War Service Western Front Fate 20.9.17 Killed in Action while serving with the 8th Infantry Battalion at Polygon Wood, Ypres, Belgium. Age at death, 28 years. No Known Grave. Commemoration The Ypres (Menin Gate) Memorial (Panel 7), Belgium. Remembrance Australian War Memorial, Panel Number 53, Roll of Honour. Recognition British War Medal. Victory Medal. Memorial Plaque. Memorial Scroll. Allendale State School Honour Board. RSL Virtual War Memorial. Avenue of Honour Tree W21 Additional Notes Worked as an accountant with the Railway Department in Melbourne. Notes 11.4.16 promoted to Corporal, with temporary promotion to Quartermaster Sergeant for duration of voyage to England. 19.6.16 disembarked Plymouth, England and immediately joined 2nd Training Battalion at Durrington Camp. Reverted to rank of Corporal from Quartermaster Sergeant. 19.6.17 proceeded overseas to France via Southampton from 2nd Training Battalion. 8.11.17 In his will, he left all of his possessions and property to his father. 27.6.18 taken on strength of 8th Battalion from 15th Reinforcements. 23.1.18 Army Report concluded that soldier had been Killed in Action in the field of combat in Belgium. 31.12.18 Creswick Advertiser published a list of all from the Borough and Shire who had been Killed in Action including Corporal Johannsen. Family Military Connections Brother, Henry Neiman Johannsen, [No. 6946], 8th Infantry Battalion, returned to Australia 4.9.17. Cousin, Rupert Claude Johannsen, [No. 279], 29th Infantry Battalion, returned to Australia 13.4.19.",
