    "militaryMedalCitation": "",
    "fullProfile": "Jones, Thomas Charles Family Born Cragie (near Majorca) 1893. Birth Registration 1893/5406. Father: Lewis David Jones. Mother: Mary Ann (Annie) Driver. Married Lillian May Dean 1919. Marriage Registration 1919/11171. Enlistment Joined 19.7.15 Melbourne. Service number: 3795. Rank: Private. Roll title: 14th Infantry Battalion, 5th to 12th Reinforcements, (April to November 1915). Age on enlistment: 22 years. Occupation: Grocer’s Assistant. Marital status: Single. Religion: Methodist. Next of kin and address: father, Lewis David Jones, Hagelthorn Street, Wonthaggi. Embarkation Embarked Melbourne 23.11.15 on ship A40 HMAT Ceramic. Rate of pay per diem before and after embarkation: Five Shillings. Original unit: 14th Battalion, 12th Reinforcement. Australian War Memorial Embarkation Roll Number: 23/31/3. Later in this publication are more details about the ships that took individuals overseas. Additional Notes On death registration, father’s first name was spelt Louis. Also, mother was referred to as Annie. Fate 8.5.19 returned to Australia on board the hospital ship HT Devanha. 17.9.19 discharged 3rd Military District as medically unfit. Died 8 June 1960 Creswick. Age at death, 67 years. Death Registration 1960/22523. Place of burial: Creswick Cemetery on 10.6.1960. Recognition 1914–15 Star. British War Medal. Victory Medal. Allendale State School Honour Board. RSL Virtual War Memorial. Avenue of Honour Tree E37 Additional Notes At the time of his death he was recorded as being a resident of Allendale and occupation storekeeper. Corporal from 11.8.16; Sergeant from 22.8.16. Notes Prior experience: Smeaton Rifle Club. 1.9.16 Wounded in Action. (Gunshot wounds to arm, back and knee.) 13.9.16 evacuated from Le Havre, France for England on board hospital ship Asturias. Admitted to 3rd Auxiliary Hospital, Dartford. 10.9.17 on command at 3rd School of Instruction Candahar Barracks Tidworth from 67th Battalion Windmill Hill England. 23.6.18 Wounded in Action. (Severe shotgun wounds to shoulder.) Treated by 4th Australian Field Ambulance, then by 47th Casualty Clearing Station. 5.7.18 admitted to 1st Australian General Hospital Rouen. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private Jones from Wonthaggi had been a District Volunteer. 31.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported all from the Borough and Shire who had been Wounded in Action during WW1 and that included Sergeant Jones. 19.3.19 discharged medically unfit with foreign object still located in right shoulder.",
