    "notes": "28.12.16 disembarked Plymouth, England, posted to 69th Australian Squadron, Royal Flying Corps. 9.1.17 as part of the 69th Australian Squadron, Royal Flying Corps, on Command at Scottish School of Fitters for a course of instruction. 17.7.17 on Command at Armaments School West Ealing, England. 17.5.18 on duty in France with 3rd Squadron. 1.8.17 appointed Air Mechanic 1st Class. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private Kay from Ballarat had been a District Volunteer. 12.10.18 Corporal Air Mechanic appointed in the field. 10.7.19 granted leave with pay until 10.10.19 to attend the British No. 1 Aircraft Salvage Depot to be trained in the maintenance and flight of Sopwith aircraft. Granted permission to proceed to Australia as a mechanic on the Great Britain to Australia flight of 1919, aiding Captain George Campbell Matthews. Birth registration records name as Dominic Thomas Kay.",
    "militaryMedalCitation": "",
    "fullProfile": "Kay, Thomas Dominick Family Born Springmount 20.10.1886. Birth Registration 1886/3924. Father: John Kay. Mother: Annetta Amelia Sonsee. Enlistment Joined 5.10.16 Melbourne. Service number: 750. Rank: Air Mechanic 2nd Class. Roll title: 2nd Australian Flying Squadron, 1st to 4th Reinforcements, (October to December 1916). Age on enlistment: 27 year 11 months. Occupation: Mechanical Engineer. Marital status: Single. Religion: Church of England. Address at enlistment: 107 Brougham Street, Ballarat. Next of kin and address: sister, Mrs. Lucy Whatmore, Doveton Street Ballarat. (Both parents deceased.) Embarkation Embarked Melbourne 25.10.16 on ship A38 HMAT Ulysses. Rate of pay per diem before embarkation: Eight Shillings. Rate of pay per diem after embarkation: Seven Shillings. Original unit: Australian Flying Corps, No. 2 Squadron, C Flight. Australian War Memorial Embarkation Roll Number: 8/5/1. War Service Western Front Fate 21.10.19 left Great Britain for Australia in a Sopwith Wallaby aircraft, designed for long flights. Despite several mishaps they flew as far as Bali, but crash landed. 10.6.20 arrived in Australia aboard the Roggeveen. 16.8.20 discharged 3rd Military District. Died 19.5.1963 Queensland. Recognition British War Medal. Victory Medal. Creswick Australian Natives Assocation Honour Board. Springmount State School Honour Board. RSL Virtual War Memorial. Avenue of Honour Tree E14 Additional Notes Worked as an engine specialist at Ronaldson and Tippett in Ballarat before the war. Family Military Connection Brother, Private Percival Mark Kay, [No. 6324], 24th Infantry Battalion, returned to Australia 24.12.18. Notes 28.12.16 disembarked Plymouth, England, posted to 69th Australian Squadron, Royal Flying Corps. 9.1.17 as part of the 69th Australian Squadron, Royal Flying Corps, on Command at Scottish School of Fitters for a course of instruction. 17.7.17 on Command at Armaments School West Ealing, England. 17.5.18 on duty in France with 3rd Squadron. 1.8.17 appointed Air Mechanic 1st Class. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private Kay from Ballarat had been a District Volunteer. 12.10.18 Corporal Air Mechanic appointed in the field. 10.7.19 granted leave with pay until 10.10.19 to attend the British No. 1 Aircraft Salvage Depot to be trained in the maintenance and flight of Sopwith aircraft. Granted permission to proceed to Australia as a mechanic on the Great Britain to Australia flight of 1919, aiding Captain George Campbell Matthews. Birth registration records name as Dominic Thomas Kay.",
