    "notes": "3.6.15 Board of Enquiry determined that Private Kean had been Killed in Action on or after 25.4.15. Originally buried in Shrapnel Valley ANZAC Cemetery, about 400 yards south-east of ANZAC Cove. 19.1.17 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private Kean had been Killed in Action. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private Kean from Parkville had been a District Volunteer. 31.12.18 Creswick Advertiser published a list of all from the Borough and Shire who had been Killed in Action including Private Kean. 18.2.21 Medals together with Memorial Scroll and Plaque were sent to Private Kean’s sister (as next of kin). 22.9.21 a photograph of the grave site was sent to Private Kean’s sister (as next of kin).",
    "militaryMedalCitation": "",
    "fullProfile": "Kean, Thomas Family Born Creswick 1881. Birth Registration 1881/8584. Father: James Kean. Mother: Alice Bowler. Enlistment Joined 19.8.14 Carlton. Service number: 237. Rank: Private. Roll title: 7th Infantry Battalion (October 1914). Age on enlistment: 33 years. Occupation: Labourer. Marital status: Single. Religion: Roman Catholic. Next of kin and address: sister, Mrs. McGlone, 14 Bayles Street, Parkville Melbourne. (Both parents deceased.) Embarkation Embarked Melbourne 19 October 1914 on ship A20 HMAT Hororata. Rate of pay per diem before and after embarkation: Five Shillings. Original unit 7th Battalion. Later in this publication are more details about the ships that took individuals overseas. Additional Note Lived at Springmount. Fate Killed in Action at Gallipoli Peninsula between 25.4.15 and 2.5.15 while serving with the 7th Infantry Battalion. Age at death, 33 years. Place of burial: Plugge’s Plateau Cemetery (Row A, Grave 4), Gallipoli, Turkey. Commemoration Australian War Memorial, Panel Number 50, Roll of Honour. Recognition 1914–15 Star. British War Medal. Victory Medal. Memorial Plaque. Memorial Scroll. Springmount State School Honour Board. RSL Virtual War Memorial. Avenue of Honour Tree E112 Notes 3.6.15 Board of Enquiry determined that Private Kean had been Killed in Action on or after 25.4.15. Originally buried in Shrapnel Valley ANZAC Cemetery, about 400 yards south-east of ANZAC Cove. 19.1.17 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private Kean had been Killed in Action. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private Kean from Parkville had been a District Volunteer. 31.12.18 Creswick Advertiser published a list of all from the Borough and Shire who had been Killed in Action including Private Kean. 18.2.21 Medals together with Memorial Scroll and Plaque were sent to Private Kean’s sister (as next of kin). 22.9.21 a photograph of the grave site was sent to Private Kean’s sister (as next of kin).",
