    "notes": "30.6.16 admitted to 2nd General Hospital Le Havre, France with contusion to thigh. 11.7.16 discharged to Battalion Depot at Etaples. 9.3.17 Wounded in Action. (Shotgun wound to back.) 22.9.17 transferred to England. Treated by Cambridge Military Hospital, Aldershot. 19.10.17 discharged to Command Depot Weymouth. 4.9.17 given 21 days’ field punishment for drunkenness, creating a disturbance and using insubordinate language to an NCO. Punishment imposed by Commanding Officer, Field Artillery Brigade. 23.1.18 proceeded overseas to France from Heytesbury. 26.1.18 joined Field Artillery Brigade. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private Kenneally from Broomfield had been a District Volunteer. 31.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported all from the Borough and Shire who had been Wounded in Action including Gunner Kenneally.",
    "militaryMedalCitation": "",
    "fullProfile": "Kenneally, John Henry Family Born Broomfield 1889. Father: James Kenneally. Mother: Mary Ann Blackman. Married Alvina Wilhelmina Rau 1915. Marriage registration 1915/11109. Enlistment Joined 23.7.1915 Geelong. Service number: 3568. Rank: Private. Roll title: 22nd Infantry Battalion, 1st to 8th Reinforcements, (June 1915 to January 1916). Age on enlistment: 27 years. Occupation: Labourer. Marital status: Married. Religion: Church of England. Next of kin and address: widowed mother, Mrs. Kenneally, Broomfield Post Office. Embarkation Embarked Melbourne 5.1.16 on ship A19 HMAT Afric. Rate of pay per diem before and after embarkation: Five Shillings. Original unit: 22nd Infantry Battalion. Later in this publication are more details about the ships that took individuals overseas. Additional Note Also known as Jack. Fate 24.1.19 returned to Australia on board the ship Delta. 24.4.19 discharged 3rd Military District as medically unfit. Died 7.4.1961 Newcombe. Age at death, 71 years. Death Registration 1961/5773. Cremated at Altona Memorial Park where cremated remains were interred. Recognition British War Medal. Victory Medal. Broomfield State School Honour Board. RSL Virtual War Memorial. Avenue of Honour Tree W9 Family Military Connections Brother, Private James Kenneally, [No. 3810], 29th Infantry Battalion, returned to Australia 15.9.18. Cousin, Private William Dunn, [No. 451], 5th Infantry Battalion, Killed in Action 25.4.15. Notes 30.6.16 admitted to 2nd General Hospital Le Havre, France with contusion to thigh. 11.7.16 discharged to Battalion Depot at Etaples. 9.3.17 Wounded in Action. (Shotgun wound to back.) 22.9.17 transferred to England. Treated by Cambridge Military Hospital, Aldershot. 19.10.17 discharged to Command Depot Weymouth. 4.9.17 given 21 days’ field punishment for drunkenness, creating a disturbance and using insubordinate language to an NCO. Punishment imposed by Commanding Officer, Field Artillery Brigade. 23.1.18 proceeded overseas to France from Heytesbury. 26.1.18 joined Field Artillery Brigade. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private Kenneally from Broomfield had been a District Volunteer. 31.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported all from the Borough and Shire who had been Wounded in Action including Gunner Kenneally.",
