    "warService": "",
    "nursingService": "",
    "familyConnection": "",
    "additionalNotes": "",
    "notes": "Final Report: “He was with others in a dugout containing about 50 boxes of Mills grenades when a shell exploded under the parapet and I saw the lot go up. He was buried near the sugar refinery back of Yarra Bank about 1 and a half or 2 miles from Flers. There were 3 other killed same time as Kilfoyle. I knew him real well. As I was with him for 3 or 4 months on observation post. We came over together from Melbourne leaving there in the Wandilla on 9th Nov 1915. The pioneers put up an ordinary wooden cross on his grave. He was 24 or 26 years old.” Report by Private. M. Gale, Service Number 210. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private Kilfoyle from Smeaton had been a District Volunteer.",
