    "notes": "10.12.15 admitted to hospital with diphtheria. Rejoined unit 8.1.16. 19.3.16 Wounded in Action. (Gunshot wounds to head and compound fracture to the skull.) Admitted to 2nd Australian Field Ambulance, Alexandria, Egypt. 17.6.16 penalised 120 hours of field punishment for overstaying leave by one hour. Penalty imposed by Commanding Officer 22nd Battalion. 28.7.16 Died of Wounds while being treated by 2nd Australian Field Ambulance. 4.10.16 Field Service Report by 2nd Australian Field Ambulance France in regard to Private Lever (from 22nd Battalion AIF) stated that he had died on 28.7.16 from wounds received in action. 17.10.16 Private Lever’s mother was granted a pension of one pound ten shillings per fortnight. 19.1.17 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private Lever had been Killed in Action. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private Lever from Allendale had been a District Volunteer.",
    "militaryMedalCitation": "",
    "fullProfile": "Lever, Francis Dysart Phelan Family Born Allendale 1891. Birth Registration 1891/29673. Father: John Henry Lever. Mother: Mary Dysart Phelan. Enlistment Joined 1.2.15 Horsham. Service number: 188. Rank: Private. Roll title: 22nd Infantry Battalion (May 1915). Age on enlistment: 23 years 9 months. Marital status: Single. Religion: Roman Catholic. Occupation: Grocer. Next of kin and address: mother, Mary Lever, Allendale. Embarkation Embarked Melbourne 10.5.15 on ship A38 HMAT Ulysses. Rate of pay per diem before and after embarkation: Five Shillings. Original unit: 22nd Battalion, A Company. Australian War Memorial Embarkation Roll Number: 23/39/1. Later in this publication are more details about the ships that took individuals overseas. Additional Notes 31.12.18 Creswick Advertiser published a list of all from the Borough and Shire who had been Killed in Action including Private Lever. 22.12.20 report indicated that there were no relatives of Private Lever now living in Allendale. Fate 28.7.16 Died of Wounds while serving with the 22nd Infantry Battalion, France. Age at death, 25 years. Place of burial: Warloy-Baillon Communal Cemetery Extension (Plot VII, Row A, Grave Number 23) France. Commemoration Australian War Memorial, Panel Number 96, Roll of Honour. Recognition 1914–15 Star. British War Medal. Victory Medal. Memorial Scroll. Memorial Plaque. Allendale State School Honour Board. Hollinwood State School Honour Board. RSL Virtual War Memorial. Avenue of Honour Tree E95 Notes 10.12.15 admitted to hospital with diphtheria. Rejoined unit 8.1.16. 19.3.16 Wounded in Action. (Gunshot wounds to head and compound fracture to the skull.) Admitted to 2nd Australian Field Ambulance, Alexandria, Egypt. 17.6.16 penalised 120 hours of field punishment for overstaying leave by one hour. Penalty imposed by Commanding Officer 22nd Battalion. 28.7.16 Died of Wounds while being treated by 2nd Australian Field Ambulance. 4.10.16 Field Service Report by 2nd Australian Field Ambulance France in regard to Private Lever (from 22nd Battalion AIF) stated that he had died on 28.7.16 from wounds received in action. 17.10.16 Private Lever’s mother was granted a pension of one pound ten shillings per fortnight. 19.1.17 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private Lever had been Killed in Action. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private Lever from Allendale had been a District Volunteer.",
