    "nursingService": "",
    "familyConnection": "",
    "additionalNotes": "His middle name is recorded as Robert.",
    "notes": "28.2.17 admitted to hospital in Etaples, France with pneumonia. 22.3.17 evacuated to England on board the Warilla from Le Havre with pleurisy. 14.7.17 Medical Board Report at 3rd Australian Auxiliary Hospital Dartford permanently unfit for general service owing to pleurisy and pneumonia. 12.1.18 Private McCormack was granted a pension of two pound five shillings per fortnight. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private McCormack from Newlyn had been a District Volunteer. 27.7.23 Base Records wrote to Australia House London informing it that Private McCormack had died from causes attributable to war service and that his widowed mother is entitled to receive the Memorial Scroll and Memorial Plaque. 27.7.23 because Private McCormack died before his medals were issued, they were sent to his mother in Ireland. Australian War Memorial advised that he was ineligible to be recognised on the Roll of Honour as his date of death fell outside the cutoff date for inclusion which was 31.3.21.",
    "militaryMedalCitation": "",
    "fullProfile": "McCormack, William Family Born Mohill, Ireland c. 1897. Father: Robert McCormack. Enlistment Joined 27.3.16 Ballarat. Service number: 1690. Rank: Private. Roll title: 39th Infantry Battalion, 1st to 6th Reinforcements, (May to December 1916). Age on enlistment: 19 years 4 months. Marital status: Single. Religion: Church of England. Address at enrolment, c/o Mr. J. J. May, Newlyn. Next of kin and address: Originally his father, Robert McCormack, Tolcon, Loughynn, Dromod County, Leitrim, Ireland. After his father passed away, next of kin was changed to his mother. Embarkation Embarked Melbourne 27.5.16 on ship A11 HMAT Ascanius. Rate of pay per diem before and after embarkation: Five Shillings. Original unit: 39th Battalion, 1st Reinforcement. Australian War Memorial Embarkation Roll Number: 23/56/2. Later in this publication are more details about the ships that took individuals overseas. Fate 25.8.17 returned to Australia. (The hospital ship on which he returned was not disclosed in his file.) 11.1.18 discharged 3rd Military District as medically unfit. 12.4.20 admitted to 11th Australian General Hospital. He died 18.5.1921 of tuberculosis and exhaustion. No relatives were present. Recognition British War Medal. Victory Medal. Memorial Scroll. Memorial Plaque. St. Matthew’s Anglican Church Newlyn Honour Board. RSL Virtual War Memorial. Avenue of Honour Tree W98 Additional Notes His middle name is recorded as Robert. Notes 28.2.17 admitted to hospital in Etaples, France with pneumonia. 22.3.17 evacuated to England on board the Warilla from Le Havre with pleurisy. 14.7.17 Medical Board Report at 3rd Australian Auxiliary Hospital Dartford permanently unfit for general service owing to pleurisy and pneumonia. 12.1.18 Private McCormack was granted a pension of two pound five shillings per fortnight. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private McCormack from Newlyn had been a District Volunteer. 27.7.23 Base Records wrote to Australia House London informing it that Private McCormack had died from causes attributable to war service and that his widowed mother is entitled to receive the Memorial Scroll and Memorial Plaque. 27.7.23 because Private McCormack died before his medals were issued, they were sent to his mother in Ireland. Australian War Memorial advised that he was ineligible to be recognised on the Roll of Honour as his date of death fell outside the cutoff date for inclusion which was 31.3.21.",
