    "name": "McKinnon, Andrew",
    "gender": "Male",
    "rank": "Private",
    "role": "Soldier",
    "location": "Kingston / Creswick",
    "service": "Joined 18 August 1914. Service Number: 323. Rank: Private. Roll title: 8th Infantry Battalion (October 1914). Age on enlistment: 28 years. Occupation: Farmer. Marital status: Single. Religion: Presbyterian. Next of kin and address: father, Mr. Andrew McKinnon, Kingston Post Office. Embarked Melbourne 19 October 1914 on ship A24 HMAT Benalla. Rate of pay per diem before and after embarkation: Five Shillings. Original unit: 8th Battalion, C Company. Australian War Memorial Embarkation Roll Number: 23/25/1. Later in this publication are more details about the ships that took individuals overseas. Family Military Connections Sister, Staff Nurse Annie McKinnon, Australian Army Nursing Service, returned to Australia 1.11.19. Sister, Staff Nurse Ruby McKinnon. [Served in Australia.] Brother, Private Norman McKinnon [No. 7553], 8th Infantry Battalion, returned to Australia, 13.7.19. Cousin, Private James Lindsay Archibald, [No. 677], Killed in Action France, 13.8.16.",
    "family": "Born Kingston 1886. Birth Registration 1886/19472. Father: Andrew McKinnon. Mother: Mary Ann Archibald.",
    "enlistment": "Joined 18 August 1914. Service Number: 323. Rank: Private. Roll title: 8th Infantry Battalion (October 1914). Age on enlistment: 28 years. Occupation: Farmer. Marital status: Single. Religion: Presbyterian. Next of kin and address: father, Mr. Andrew McKinnon, Kingston Post Office.",
    "embarkation": "Embarked Melbourne 19 October 1914 on ship A24 HMAT Benalla. Rate of pay per diem before and after embarkation: Five Shillings. Original unit: 8th Battalion, C Company. Australian War Memorial Embarkation Roll Number: 23/25/1. Later in this publication are more details about the ships that took individuals overseas. Family Military Connections Sister, Staff Nurse Annie McKinnon, Australian Army Nursing Service, returned to Australia 1.11.19. Sister, Staff Nurse Ruby McKinnon. [Served in Australia.] Brother, Private Norman McKinnon [No. 7553], 8th Infantry Battalion, returned to Australia, 13.7.19. Cousin, Private James Lindsay Archibald, [No. 677], Killed in Action France, 13.8.16.",
    "fate": "19.7.16 Killed in Action while serving with the 60th Infantry Battalion at Fromelles, France. Age at death, 29 years. No Known Grave.",
    "recognition": "Mentioned in Despatches. 1914–15 Star. British War Medal. Victory Medal. Memorial Scroll. Memorial Plaque. Kingston State School Honour Board. RSL Virtual War Memorial. Avenue of Honour Tree W1",
    "commemoration": "V.C. Corner (Panel No. 18), Australian Cemetery Memorial, Fromelles, France. Remembrance Australian War Memorial, Panel Number 170, Roll of Honour.",
    "warService": "Egypt; Gallipoli; Turkey; Western Front",
    "nursingService": "",
    "familyConnection": "",
    "additionalNotes": "",
    "notes": "Private from 18.8.14; Corporal from 12.5.15; Sergeant from 13.8.15; Second Lieutenant from 17.6.16. 22.8.15 Wounded in Action at Anzac Cove. (Gunshot wound to hand.) Treated by 2nd Casualty Clearing Station before being evacuated to England on hospital ship Gloucester Castle. 30.8.15 transferred to Alexandria and admitted to 2nd Australian General Hospital, Ghezireh. 16.10.15 Wounded in Action. (Shrapnel wound to right knee.) Admitted to Mena House Hospital. 29.10.15 admitted to 2nd Stationary Hospital, Mudros, Egypt. 6.11.15 discharged. 29.2.16 mentioned in Despatches for services rendered at Gallipoli. Creswick Advertiser reported that he was Missing in Action from 19.7.16. 30.8.16 reported as Missing in Action. 29.6.17 Board of Enquiry determination that he had been Killed in Action 19.7.16 at Fromelles. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private McKinnon from Kingston had been a District Volunteer. 31.12.18 Creswick Advertiser published a list of all from the Borough and Shire who had been Killed in Action including Lieutenant McKinnon.",
    "militaryMedalCitation": "",
    "fullProfile": "McKinnon, Andrew Family Born Kingston 1886. Birth Registration 1886/19472. Father: Andrew McKinnon. Mother: Mary Ann Archibald. Enlistment Joined 18 August 1914. Service Number: 323. Rank: Private. Roll title: 8th Infantry Battalion (October 1914). Age on enlistment: 28 years. Occupation: Farmer. Marital status: Single. Religion: Presbyterian. Next of kin and address: father, Mr. Andrew McKinnon, Kingston Post Office. Embarkation Embarked Melbourne 19 October 1914 on ship A24 HMAT Benalla. Rate of pay per diem before and after embarkation: Five Shillings. Original unit: 8th Battalion, C Company. Australian War Memorial Embarkation Roll Number: 23/25/1. Later in this publication are more details about the ships that took individuals overseas. Family Military Connections Sister, Staff Nurse Annie McKinnon, Australian Army Nursing Service, returned to Australia 1.11.19. Sister, Staff Nurse Ruby McKinnon. [Served in Australia.] Brother, Private Norman McKinnon [No. 7553], 8th Infantry Battalion, returned to Australia, 13.7.19. Cousin, Private James Lindsay Archibald, [No. 677], Killed in Action France, 13.8.16. Fate 19.7.16 Killed in Action while serving with the 60th Infantry Battalion at Fromelles, France. Age at death, 29 years. No Known Grave. Commemoration V.C. Corner (Panel No. 18), Australian Cemetery Memorial, Fromelles, France. Remembrance Australian War Memorial, Panel Number 170, Roll of Honour. Recognition Mentioned in Despatches. 1914–15 Star. British War Medal. Victory Medal. Memorial Scroll. Memorial Plaque. Kingston State School Honour Board. RSL Virtual War Memorial. Avenue of Honour Tree W1 War Service Egypt; Gallipoli; Turkey; Western Front Notes Private from 18.8.14; Corporal from 12.5.15; Sergeant from 13.8.15; Second Lieutenant from 17.6.16. 22.8.15 Wounded in Action at Anzac Cove. (Gunshot wound to hand.) Treated by 2nd Casualty Clearing Station before being evacuated to England on hospital ship Gloucester Castle. 30.8.15 transferred to Alexandria and admitted to 2nd Australian General Hospital, Ghezireh. 16.10.15 Wounded in Action. (Shrapnel wound to right knee.) Admitted to Mena House Hospital. 29.10.15 admitted to 2nd Stationary Hospital, Mudros, Egypt. 6.11.15 discharged. 29.2.16 mentioned in Despatches for services rendered at Gallipoli. Creswick Advertiser reported that he was Missing in Action from 19.7.16. 30.8.16 reported as Missing in Action. 29.6.17 Board of Enquiry determination that he had been Killed in Action 19.7.16 at Fromelles. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private McKinnon from Kingston had been a District Volunteer. 31.12.18 Creswick Advertiser published a list of all from the Borough and Shire who had been Killed in Action including Lieutenant McKinnon.",
