    "embarkation": "Although she enlisted for active service, she was not sent abroad as there were several applicants in front of her and the war ceased.",
    "fate": "28.8.19 demobilised in Melbourne. After discharge, she returned to nursing in Ballarat and living at 40 Pleasant Street. Died 18.9.1954 Ballarat at the age of 65 years. Death Registration 1954/20238. Place of burial: Creswick Cemetery on 20.9.1954.",
    "recognition": "Kingston State School Honour Board. Avenue of Honour Tree W41",
    "commemoration": "",
    "warService": "",
    "nursingService": "",
    "familyConnection": "",
    "additionalNotes": "At the time of her death, she was recorded as being a resident of Ballarat with an occupation not recorded in Probate documentation. Family Military Connections Brother, Private Andrew Charles McKinnon, [No. 323], 8th Infantry Battalion, Killed in Action 19.7.16. Brother, Private Norman McKinnon, [No. 7553], 8th Infantry Battalion, returned to Australia 13.7.19. Sister, Staff Nurse Annie McKinnon, returned to Australia 1.11.19. Cousin, Private James Lindsay Archibald, [No. 677], 14th Infantry Battalion, Killed in Action France, 13.8.16.",
    "notes": "14.1.16 Creswick Advertiser reported on the successful completion of the nursing exams by Annie McKinnon and that her sister Ruby was still in Training at Bendigo Hospital. 27.12.16 The Argus reported on successful candidates at the examination held in November 1916 by the Royal Victorian Trained Nurses Association and that included Ruby McKinnon from Bendigo Hospital. 9.10.17 Creswick Advertiser reported on a farewell function for Sister Ruby McKinnon and a small number of other volunteers. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Staff Nurse McKinnon from Kingston had been a District Volunteer. 7.7.20 The Local Repatriation Committee, Ballarat, approved an irregular grant of 9 pounds, 17 shillings for tools of trade. In 1920 she was sharing a house with her sisters Annie and Flora (also known as Tot). She and Annie were still nursing, and Flora was recorded as housekeeper. In 1920, the Repatriation Department approved a benefit application on the basis that she had enlisted for active service and was still on duty at the time of the Armistice. Value of benefit not recorded.",
    "militaryMedalCitation": "",
    "fullProfile": "McKinnon, Sister Ruby Family Born 1889 Kingston. Birth Registration 1889/13722. Father: Andrew McKinnon. Mother: Mary Ann Archibald. Married Thomas Robinson 1933. Marriage Registration 1933/8425. Enlistment Joined 13.6.17 Melbourne. Rank: Staff Nurse. Unit: Australian Army Nursing Service, No. 5 Australian General Hospital. Age on enlistment: 28 years. Occupation: Trained Nurse. Marital status: Single. Religion: Presbyterian. Next of kin and address: father, Mr. A. McKinnon, Kingston Post Office. Embarkation Although she enlisted for active service, she was not sent abroad as there were several applicants in front of her and the war ceased. Additional Notes Middle name Margaret. On the 1917 Electoral Roll, she was recorded as living at the Bendigo Hospital, occupation nurse. In 1919, she was back living in Kingston and still working as a nurse. Length of service 2 years and 2 months with both the No. 5 and No. 11 Australian General Hospitals. Fate 28.8.19 demobilised in Melbourne. After discharge, she returned to nursing in Ballarat and living at 40 Pleasant Street. Died 18.9.1954 Ballarat at the age of 65 years. Death Registration 1954/20238. Place of burial: Creswick Cemetery on 20.9.1954. Recognition Kingston State School Honour Board. Avenue of Honour Tree W41 Additional Notes At the time of her death, she was recorded as being a resident of Ballarat with an occupation not recorded in Probate documentation. Family Military Connections Brother, Private Andrew Charles McKinnon, [No. 323], 8th Infantry Battalion, Killed in Action 19.7.16. Brother, Private Norman McKinnon, [No. 7553], 8th Infantry Battalion, returned to Australia 13.7.19. Sister, Staff Nurse Annie McKinnon, returned to Australia 1.11.19. Cousin, Private James Lindsay Archibald, [No. 677], 14th Infantry Battalion, Killed in Action France, 13.8.16. Notes 14.1.16 Creswick Advertiser reported on the successful completion of the nursing exams by Annie McKinnon and that her sister Ruby was still in Training at Bendigo Hospital. 27.12.16 The Argus reported on successful candidates at the examination held in November 1916 by the Royal Victorian Trained Nurses Association and that included Ruby McKinnon from Bendigo Hospital. 9.10.17 Creswick Advertiser reported on a farewell function for Sister Ruby McKinnon and a small number of other volunteers. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Staff Nurse McKinnon from Kingston had been a District Volunteer. 7.7.20 The Local Repatriation Committee, Ballarat, approved an irregular grant of 9 pounds, 17 shillings for tools of trade. In 1920 she was sharing a house with her sisters Annie and Flora (also known as Tot). She and Annie were still nursing, and Flora was recorded as housekeeper. In 1920, the Repatriation Department approved a benefit application on the basis that she had enlisted for active service and was still on duty at the time of the Armistice. Value of benefit not recorded.",
    "locDesc": "West side of Kingston Road. Tree W41.",
    "lat": -37.41025,
