    "fullProfile": "McMillan, Henry Alexander Family Born Kingston 1894. Birth Registration 1894/17053. Father: Oscar McMillan. Mother: Agnes Williams. Enlistment Joined 27.7.1915 Melbourne. Service number: 4264. Rank: Private. Roll title: 14th Infantry Battalion, 13th Reinforcement. Age on enlistment: 21 years. Occupation: Labourer. Marital status: Single. Religion: Church of England. Next of kin and address: father, Oscar McMillan, Allendale Post Office. Embarkation Embarked Melbourne 29.12.15 on ship A64 HMAT Demosthernes. Original unit: 14th Infantry Battalion. Australian War Memorial Embarkation Roll Number: 23/31/4. Later in this publication are more details about the ships that took individuals overseas. Additional Notes Was employed by John Leishman as a farm assistant. Fate 11.4.17 Killed in Action while serving with the 46th Battalion at Bullecourt. No Known Grave. Commemoration Australian National Memorial, Villiers- Bretonneux. Remembrance Australian War Memorial, Panel Number 142, Roll of Honour. Recognition British War Medal. Victory Medal. Memorial Plaque. Memorial Scroll. Allendale State School Honour Board. RSL Virtual War Memorial. Avenue of Honour Tree E38 Notes Prior experience: 3 years with 71st Infantry. 10.6.16 treated by 12th Field Ambulance for influenza before being transferred to 12th Casualty Clearing Station Merris, France. Admitted to 2nd Australian General Hospital Mousset for 2 weeks. 11.4.17 first reported as Missing in Action. 26.11.17 Court of Enquiry determined that Private McMillan had been Killed in Action. Also recorded that his will had not been received. 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private McMillan from Wallacetown had been a District Volunteer. 31.12.18 Creswick Advertiser published a list of all from the Borough and Shire who had been Killed in Action including Private McMillan. Cemetery details from AIF record his name as Henry Alexander MacMillan. In some records he was known as William Henry McMillan.",
