    "additionalNotes": "3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private May from Newlyn had been a District Volunteer. 31.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported all from the Borough and Shire who had been Wounded in Action during WW1 and that included Private May.",
    "notes": "Prior experience: 2 years with 13th Australian Light Horse Regiment. 16.5.15 proceeded to join Mesopotanium Expeditionary Forces on Gallipoli Peninsula. 7.9.15 Wounded in Action. (Listed as dangerously ill with gunshot wounds to chest.) Admitted to 17th General Hospital in Alexandria, Egypt and then transferred to 4th London General Hospital Denmark Hill, England for further treatment. Later classified as a severe lung wound. 11.9.16 detached for duty with AIF Headquarters Depot. 27.4.17 taken on strength from AIF Headquarters to Reserve Brigade Australian Artillery, Heytesbury. 4.5.18 proceeded overseas to France via Southampton, with 9th Light Horse. 19.5.18 taken on strength of 2nd Division Artillery Corps in the field. 5.12.18 granted special leave to return home to Australia, due to Termination of Period of Enlistment.",
    "militaryMedalCitation": "",
    "fullProfile": "May, Sydney John Family Born Newlyn 15.3.1895. Birth Registration 1895/14564. Father: Duncan May. Mother: Elizabeth Ann Russell. Married Ruby Linaker 1921. Marriage Registration 1921/8704. Enlistment Joined 14.9.14 Stratford. Service number: 604. Rank: Private. Roll title: 9th Light Horse Regiment, 1st Reinforcement. Age on enlistment: 21 years. Occupation: Labourer. Marital status: Single. Religion: Church of England. Next of kin and address: W. E. Lestrange, Bushy Park Victoria. Embarkation Embarked Melbourne 6.2.15 on ship A52 HMAT Surada. Original unit: 5th Field Artillery. Australian War Memorial Embarkation Roll Number: 10/14/2. Later in this publication are more details about the ships that took individuals overseas. Additional Notes According to Victorian Public Records, he was born John Sydney, married and listed on Honour Boards as Sydney John, and died John Sydney. Final Rank: Gunner. Fate 14.12.18 returned to Australia aboard the ship HT Burmah. 30.3.19 discharged 3rd Military District. Died 10.2.1971 Heidelberg. Age at death, 75 years. Buried 12.2.71 Springvale Cemetary. Death Registration 1971/3730. Recognition 1914–15 Star. British War Medal. Victory Medal. RSL Virtual War Memorial. Avenue of Honour Tree E23 Additional Notes 3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported that Private May from Newlyn had been a District Volunteer. 31.12.18 Creswick Advertiser reported all from the Borough and Shire who had been Wounded in Action during WW1 and that included Private May. Notes Prior experience: 2 years with 13th Australian Light Horse Regiment. 16.5.15 proceeded to join Mesopotanium Expeditionary Forces on Gallipoli Peninsula. 7.9.15 Wounded in Action. (Listed as dangerously ill with gunshot wounds to chest.) Admitted to 17th General Hospital in Alexandria, Egypt and then transferred to 4th London General Hospital Denmark Hill, England for further treatment. Later classified as a severe lung wound. 11.9.16 detached for duty with AIF Headquarters Depot. 27.4.17 taken on strength from AIF Headquarters to Reserve Brigade Australian Artillery, Heytesbury. 4.5.18 proceeded overseas to France via Southampton, with 9th Light Horse. 19.5.18 taken on strength of 2nd Division Artillery Corps in the field. 5.12.18 granted special leave to return home to Australia, due to Termination of Period of Enlistment.",
