    "notes": "28.7.15 Wounded in Action. Dardenelles. (Gunshot wound to abdomen.) Transported by hospital ship to Malta where he was admitted to St. David’s Hospital Valletta on 28.8.15. 18.9.15 transported on hospital ship Oxfordshire from Malta to England. 24.9.15 admitted to St. George’s Hospital Stamford. 4.3.16 discharged from hospital and proceeded to France to rejoin unit. 16.3.16 taken on strength with 4th Pioneer Battalion at Tel-el-Kebir. 24.7.16 penalised 7 days’ field punishment for disobeying company orders in the field. 7.2.17 temporary promotion to Lance Corporal. Formally promoted 20.5.17. 22.8.17 detached for duty with Training Battalion, Fovant Camp England. 4.10.17 on Command at Gunnery School of Instruction, Reading. 16.11.17 rejoined Training Battalion. 2.3.18 proceeded overseas to France from Sutton Veny Camp. 23.8.18 promoted to Sergeant. 8.10.18 returned to Australia via Italy on special leave.",
    "militaryMedalCitation": "",
    "fullProfile": "Merritt, Arthur Family Born Broomfield 1890. Birth Registration 1890/20169, father, Jesse Merritt. Mother, Mary Ann Barker. Married Amelia Adele Jayne 1920, Marriage Registration 1920/14172. Enlistment Joined 10.9.14 Creswick. Service number, 900. Rank, Private. Roll title: 14th Infantry Battalion AIF. Age on enlistment: 24 years. Occupation, Labourer. Marital Status, Single. Religion, Church of England. Next of kin and address, mother, Mrs. Mary Ann Merritt, North Creswick. Embarkation Embarked Melbourne 22.12.14 on ship A38 HMAT Ulysses. Rate of pay per diem before and after Embarkation Five Shillings. Original unit: 14th Infantry Battalion AIF. Australian War Memorial Embarkation Roll Number 23/31/1. Later in this publication are more details about the ships that took individuals overseas. Family Military Connection Brother, Private Jesse Merritt, [No. 7266], 6th Battalion, 24th Reinforcement, returned to Australia 2.1.19. Fate 8.10.18 returned to Australia on board the Kaiser-a-Hind. 31.1.19 discharged 3rd Military District. Died 10.10.63 Ballarat. Death Registration 1963/21734. Age at death, 73 years. His remains were cremated on 11.10.63 but were not interred at Creswick Cemetery until 15.1.85. Recognition 1914-1915 Star. British War Medal. Victory Medal. RSL Virtual War Memorial. Avenue of Honour Tree E83 Additional Notes Unit on Nominal Roll, 4th Australian Pioneer Battalion. After returning home he was granted a soldier settlement block of land which he paid off in 1943. Notes 28.7.15 Wounded in Action. Dardenelles. (Gunshot wound to abdomen.) Transported by hospital ship to Malta where he was admitted to St. David’s Hospital Valletta on 28.8.15. 18.9.15 transported on hospital ship Oxfordshire from Malta to England. 24.9.15 admitted to St. George’s Hospital Stamford. 4.3.16 discharged from hospital and proceeded to France to rejoin unit. 16.3.16 taken on strength with 4th Pioneer Battalion at Tel-el-Kebir. 24.7.16 penalised 7 days’ field punishment for disobeying company orders in the field. 7.2.17 temporary promotion to Lance Corporal. Formally promoted 20.5.17. 22.8.17 detached for duty with Training Battalion, Fovant Camp England. 4.10.17 on Command at Gunnery School of Instruction, Reading. 16.11.17 rejoined Training Battalion. 2.3.18 proceeded overseas to France from Sutton Veny Camp. 23.8.18 promoted to Sergeant. 8.10.18 returned to Australia via Italy on special leave.",
