    "notes": "6.3.16 joined 46th\nBattalion\nfrom 14th\nTraining Battalion\nat Zeitoun.\n2.6.16 departed Alexandria\nEgypt, to join BEF [British\nExpeditionary Force] France.\n8.6.16 arrived Marseilles.\n22.1.17 reported sick to 7th\nAustralian Field Ambulance.\nTransferred to 39th\nCasualty\nClearing Station with\nmumps. 29.1.17 admitted to\n8th\nStationary Hospital\nWimereux, France. 13.2.17\nevacuated to England.\n26.2.17 discharged to unit.\n6.2.18 to 23.2.18 on leave to\nEngland.\n30.5.18 forfeited four days'\npay for being absent without\npermission from billeted\narea.\n25.8.18 Wounded in Action.\n(Gassed.) Treated by 12th\nAustralian Field Ambulance.\nTransferred to 55th\nCasualty\nClearing Station. 31.8.18\nadmitted to 41st\nStationary\nHospital in the field.\n10.10.18 rejoined Battalion.\n3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser\nreported that Private\nNorman from Newlyn had\nbeen a District Volunteer.\nAt the time of his death, he\nwas recorded as being a\nresident of Ballarat and\noccupation blacksmith.",
    "militaryMedalCitation": "",
    "fullProfile": "Norman, Clarence Albert\nFamily\nBorn Newlyn 1893. Birth\nRegistration 1893/28585. Father:\nJohn Nunn Norman. Mother:\nGertrude (Emma Jane)\nCupples. Married Elsie May\nTrigg 1915. Marriage Registration\n1915/12698.\nEnlistment\nJoined 15.7.15 Ballarat.\nService number: 4275. Rank:\nPrivate. Roll title: 14th\nBattalion, 13th\nReinforcement. Age on\nenlistment: 21 years.\nOccupation: Blacksmith.\nMarital status: Married.\nReligion: Church of England.\nNext of kin and address:\nfather, John Norman,\nNewlyn Post Office via\nCreswick.\nEmbarkation\nEmbarked Melbourne\n29.12.15 on ship A64 HMAT\nDemosthenes. Original unit:\n14th\nBattalion. Australian\nWar Memorial Embarkation\nRoll Number: 23/31/4.\nLater in this publication are more\ndetails about the ships that took\nindividuals overseas.\nAdditional Notes\nAlso served in World War 2,\nbased in Australia, training\nBlacksmiths for the war.\n4.7.1940 – 12.2.46 Citizen\nMilitary Forces.\nFate\n5.4.19 returned to Australia\non board the ship Armagh\nfrom Devonport.\n2.9.19 discharged 3rd\nMilitary\nDistrict.\nDied 18.4.61 Ballarat. Age at\ndeath, 67 years. Death\nRegistration 1961/7636. Place of\nburial: Ballarat (New)\nCemetery.\nRecognition\n• British War Medal.\n• Victory Medal.\n• Newlyn State School\nHonour Board.\n• Newlyn Methodist\nChurch Honour Board.\n• RSL Virtual War\nMemorial.\nAvenue of Honour Tree E51\nAdditional Notes\n27.1.19 embarked France for\nLondon in preparation for\nreturn to Australia.\nHis mother was a midwife;\nhis parents were known as\nGrans and Pops Norman.\nNotes\n6.3.16 joined 46th\nBattalion\nfrom 14th\nTraining Battalion\nat Zeitoun.\n2.6.16 departed Alexandria\nEgypt, to join BEF [British\nExpeditionary Force] France.\n8.6.16 arrived Marseilles.\n22.1.17 reported sick to 7th\nAustralian Field Ambulance.\nTransferred to 39th\nCasualty\nClearing Station with\nmumps. 29.1.17 admitted to\n8th\nStationary Hospital\nWimereux, France. 13.2.17\nevacuated to England.\n26.2.17 discharged to unit.\n6.2.18 to 23.2.18 on leave to\nEngland.\n30.5.18 forfeited four days'\npay for being absent without\npermission from billeted\narea.\n25.8.18 Wounded in Action.\n(Gassed.) Treated by 12th\nAustralian Field Ambulance.\nTransferred to 55th\nCasualty\nClearing Station. 31.8.18\nadmitted to 41st\nStationary\nHospital in the field.\n10.10.18 rejoined Battalion.\n3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser\nreported that Private\nNorman from Newlyn had\nbeen a District Volunteer.\nAt the time of his death, he\nwas recorded as being a\nresident of Ballarat and\noccupation blacksmith.",
