    "fullProfile": "Packham, Herbert Clark\nFamily\nBorn Broomfield 16.4.1895.\nBirth Registration 1895/11362.\nFather: Thomas Packham.\nMother: Margaret Jane\nClark. Married Ann Monica\nFlannagan 1927. Marriage\nRegistration 1927/159.\nEnlistment\nJoined 24.5.16 Ballarat.\nService number: 2128. Rank:\nPrivate. Roll title: 39th\nInfantry Battalion, 1st\nto 6th\nReinforcements (May to\nDecember 1916). Age on\nenlistment: 21 years 1\nmonth. Occupation: Farmer.\nMarital status: Single.\nReligion: Methodist. Next of\nkin and address: mother,\nMrs. Margaret Jane\nPackham, Broomfield.\nEmbarkation\nEmbarked Melbourne\n25.9.16 on ship A9 HMAT\nShropshire. Rate of pay per\ndiem before and after\nembarkation: Five Shillings.\nOriginal unit: 39th\nBattalion,\n3rd\nReinforcement.\nAustralian War Memorial\nEmbarkation Roll Number:\n23/56/2.\nAdditional Notes\nIn some official records,\nplace of birth and next of kin\nare incorrectly recorded as\nBloomfield rather than\nBroomfield.\nFate\n27.4.19 returned to Australia\non board the ship HMAT\nRunic from England.\n18.7.19 discharged 3rd\nMilitary District.\nDied 4.6.82 Ballarat. Age at\ndeath, 84 years. Death\nRegistration 1982/12758. Place of\nburial: Ballarat (New)\nCemetery.\nRecognition\n• British War Medal.\n• Victory Medal.\n• Creswick State School\nHonour Board.\n• Broomfield State School\nHonour Board.\n• RSL Virtual War\nMemorial.\nAvenue of Honour Tree E71\nAdditional Notes\nAt the time of his death, he\nwas recorded as being a\nresident of Wendouree and\noccupation retired secretary.\nLater in this publication are more\ndetails about the ships that took\nindividuals overseas.\nNotes\nPrior experience: 3 years\nwith Citizen Forces.\n20.12.16 proceeded from\nFolkestone to France on\nboard the Princess Victoria.\n15.1.17 taken on strength\nwith 39th\nBattalion at\nEtaples, France.\n7.6.17 Wounded in Action.\n(Gassed.) 13.6.17 admitted\nto General Military Hospital,\nFort Pitt, Chatham, London.\n30.7.17 transferred to 3rd\nAuxiliary Hospital.\n28.8.17 marched out to No.\n4 Command Depot, Codford.\n21.9.17 at 10th\nTraining\nBattalion, Durrington.\n3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser\nreported that Private\nPackham from Broomfield\nhad been a District\nVolunteer.\n31.12.18 Creswick Advertiser\nreported all from the\nBorough and Shire who had\nbeen Wounded in Action\nduring WW1 and that\nincluded Private Packham.\nFamily Military Connections\nBrother, Private James\nThomas Packham, [No.\n3229], 24th\nInfantry\nBattalion. returned to\nAustralia 3.1.19.",
