    "enlistment": "Joined 17.7.15 Melbourne.\nService number: 3132. Rank:\nPrivate. Roll title: 6th\nInfantry\nBattalion, 10th\nto 12th\nReinforcements (September\nto November 1915). Age on\nenlistment: 21 years 11\nmonths. Occupation:\nButcher. Marital status:\nSingle. Religion: Methodist.\nNext of kin and address:\nfather, James Rachinger,\nBroomfield.",
    "embarkation": "Embarked Melbourne\n29.9.15 on ship RMS\nOsterley. Rate of pay per\ndiem before and after\nembarkation: Five Shillings.\nOriginal unit: 6th\nBattalion,\n10th\nReinforcement.\nAustralian War Memorial\nEmbarkation Roll Number:\n23/23/3.\nLater in this publication are more\ndetails about the ships that took\nindividuals overseas.",
    "fate": "24.12.18 returned to\nAustralia on board the ship\nBakara.\n5.4.19 as an officer his\nservices were dispensed with\nas he was declared unfit for\nany further military service.\nDied 8.12.1942 Hampton.\nAge at death, 48 years. Death\nRegistration 1942/8838.",
    "recognition": "• Military Cross\n• 1914–15 Star.\n• British War Medal.\n• Victory Medal.\n• Broomfield State School\nHonour Board.\n• RSL Virtual War\nMemorial.\nAvenue of Honour Tree W97\nMilitary Cross Citation:\n“For conspicuous gallantry\nand devotion to duty on the\nnight of 9th\n/10th\nJuly 1918\nnear Merris.”\n[The writing on file is difficult to\nread but it would appear that\nduring an attack, a section to his\nleft had lost their direction. He\napparently, under fire on more\nthan one occasion, left his\nsoldiers to make contact with his\nleft. In so doing, he was able to\nreconstitute a continuous front\nline that aided in the success of\nthe attack against the enemy.]",
    "commemoration": "",
    "warService": "",
    "nursingService": "",
    "familyConnection": "",
    "additionalNotes": "Worked in the family\nbutcher business at\nBroomfield, formerly\nHetherington's Butchers.",
    "notes": "Prior experience: Citizen\nForces 71st\nInfantry and 2\nyears with Light Horse.\nCorporal 22.3.16;\nSergeant from 31.7.16;\n2nd\nLieutenant from 1.3.17;\nLieutenant from 11.7.17.\n13.6.16 Wounded in Action.\n(Nature of wounding not\ndisclosed but Private\nremained on duty.)\n23.8.18 Wounded in Action.\n(Severe gunshot wound to\nback and chest.) Treated by\n53rd\nCasualty Clearing Station\nbefore being admitted to 8th\nGeneral Hospital Rouen,\nFrance.\n13.10.16 Creswick Advertiser\nreported that Private\nRachinger had been\nWounded in Action.\n3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser\nreported that Private\nRachinger from Broomfield\nhad been a District\nVolunteer.\n31.12.18 Creswick Advertiser\nreported all from the\nBorough and Shire who had\nbeen Wounded in Action\nduring WW1 and that\nincluded Lieutenant\nRachinger.\n15.5.19 Medical Board\ndetermined that he was unfit\nfor active duty.",
    "militaryMedalCitation": "",
    "fullProfile": "Rachinger, Alfred William\nFamily\nBorn Broomfield 1893. Birth\nRegistration 1893/19984. Father:\nJames Augustus Rachinger.\nMother: Alice Hetherington.\nMarried Selina (Lina) Mary\nWoods 1920. Marriage\nRegistration 1920/9398.\nEnlistment\nJoined 17.7.15 Melbourne.\nService number: 3132. Rank:\nPrivate. Roll title: 6th\nInfantry\nBattalion, 10th\nto 12th\nReinforcements (September\nto November 1915). Age on\nenlistment: 21 years 11\nmonths. Occupation:\nButcher. Marital status:\nSingle. Religion: Methodist.\nNext of kin and address:\nfather, James Rachinger,\nBroomfield.\nEmbarkation\nEmbarked Melbourne\n29.9.15 on ship RMS\nOsterley. Rate of pay per\ndiem before and after\nembarkation: Five Shillings.\nOriginal unit: 6th\nBattalion,\n10th\nReinforcement.\nAustralian War Memorial\nEmbarkation Roll Number:\n23/23/3.\nLater in this publication are more\ndetails about the ships that took\nindividuals overseas.\nAdditional Notes\nWorked in the family\nbutcher business at\nBroomfield, formerly\nHetherington's Butchers.\nFate\n24.12.18 returned to\nAustralia on board the ship\nBakara.\n5.4.19 as an officer his\nservices were dispensed with\nas he was declared unfit for\nany further military service.\nDied 8.12.1942 Hampton.\nAge at death, 48 years. Death\nRegistration 1942/8838.\nRecognition\n• Military Cross\n• 1914–15 Star.\n• British War Medal.\n• Victory Medal.\n• Broomfield State School\nHonour Board.\n• RSL Virtual War\nMemorial.\nAvenue of Honour Tree W97\nMilitary Cross Citation:\n“For conspicuous gallantry\nand devotion to duty on the\nnight of 9th\n/10th\nJuly 1918\nnear Merris.”\n[The writing on file is difficult to\nread but it would appear that\nduring an attack, a section to his\nleft had lost their direction. He\napparently, under fire on more\nthan one occasion, left his\nsoldiers to make contact with his\nleft. In so doing, he was able to\nreconstitute a continuous front\nline that aided in the success of\nthe attack against the enemy.]\nNotes\nPrior experience: Citizen\nForces 71st\nInfantry and 2\nyears with Light Horse.\nCorporal 22.3.16;\nSergeant from 31.7.16;\n2nd\nLieutenant from 1.3.17;\nLieutenant from 11.7.17.\n13.6.16 Wounded in Action.\n(Nature of wounding not\ndisclosed but Private\nremained on duty.)\n23.8.18 Wounded in Action.\n(Severe gunshot wound to\nback and chest.) Treated by\n53rd\nCasualty Clearing Station\nbefore being admitted to 8th\nGeneral Hospital Rouen,\nFrance.\n13.10.16 Creswick Advertiser\nreported that Private\nRachinger had been\nWounded in Action.\n3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser\nreported that Private\nRachinger from Broomfield\nhad been a District\nVolunteer.\n31.12.18 Creswick Advertiser\nreported all from the\nBorough and Shire who had\nbeen Wounded in Action\nduring WW1 and that\nincluded Lieutenant\nRachinger.\n15.5.19 Medical Board\ndetermined that he was unfit\nfor active duty.",
