    "fate": "15.6.19 returned to Australia\non board the ship HT\nSwakopmund.\n9.9.19 discharged 3rd\nMilitary\nDistrict.\nDied 21.10.1970 Ballarat. Age\nat death, 82 years. Death\nRegistration 1970/26125. Place of\nburial: Ballarat (New)\nCemetery on 23.10.1970.",
    "recognition": "• Military Medal.\n• Distinguished Conduct\nMedal.\n• Meritorious Service\nMedal.\n• 1914–15 Star.\n• British War Medal.\n• Victory Medal.\n• Springmount State School\nHonour Board.\n• RSL Virtual War Memorial.\nAvenue of Honour Tree W88\nMilitary Medal Citation:\n“During the advance on 8th\nAugust 1918 near Morcourt, this\nNCO was in charge of a MG\n(machine gun). When the\nadvance was checked by enemy\nMG fire he showed great bravery\nin bringing his gun into action\nunder heavy fire and covering the\ninfantry advance and enabling\nthem to get up close and rush the\nenemy's position. By his\npromptitude and bravery he\nsaved heavy losses among the\ninfantry.”",
    "commemoration": "",
    "warService": "",
    "nursingService": "",
    "familyConnection": "",
    "additionalNotes": "Corporal from 26.2.17;\nSergeant from 24.9.18.",
    "notes": "25.5.17 joined 43rd\nDivision\nMachine Gun Company.\n18.6.18 awarded Meritorious\nService Medal.\n3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser\nreported that Private\nRedford from Springmount\nhad been a District\nVolunteer.\nDistinguished Conduct\nMedal Citation:\n“For marked gallantry during\nthe attack near Le Verguier,\n18th September, 1918. He took\ncommand and led his section\nbringing his guns into action\nand inflicting heavy casualties\non the enemy.” Source:\nCommonwealth Gazette No. 42,\ndated 20.5.1920.\nMeritorious Service Medal\nCitation:\n“During the period 23rd\nSeptember, 1917 to 24th\nFebruary, 1918, this NCO [non-\ncommissioned officer] has done\npersistent good work. At all\ntimes cheerful, he has led his\nmen in several actions with\nconspicuous bravery. He has\nshown marked ability in\ncommanding his men, and has\nproved a tower of strength\nunder very trying and\ndangerous circumstances.”\nSource: Commonwealth\nGazette No. 110, dated\n25.7.1918.\nAt the time of his death, he\nwas recorded as being a\nresident of Ballarat and\noccupation retired railway\nemployee.",
