    "fullProfile": "Redman, Frederick\nFamily\nBorn Broomfield 1890. Birth\nRegistration 1890/30100. Father:\nEdwin Joseph Redman.\nMother: Elizabeth Barbara\nDalgleish. Married Leila\nQuanchi 1921. Marriage\nRegistration 1921/3736.\nEnlistment\nJoined 8.7.15 Melbourne.\nService number: 7997. Rank:\nActing Corporal. Roll title: 8th\nInfantry Brigade Train, 18th\nCompany ASC [Army Service\nCorps] (November 1915).\nAge on enlistment: 24 years\n10 months. Occupation:\nDealer. Marital status:\nSingle. Religion: Church of\nEngland. Next of kin and\naddress: father, Edwin J.\nRedman, Broomfield via\nCreswick.\nEmbarkation\nEmbarked Melbourne\n5.11.15 on ship A41 HMAT\nBakara. Rate of pay per diem\nbefore and after\nembarkation: Six Shillings.\nOriginal unit: 8th\nInfantry\nBrigade Train. Australian\nWar Memorial Embarkation\nRoll Number: 25/37/1.\nFamily Military Connection\nBrother, Private Albert John\nRedman, [No. 7972], 8th\nInfantry Brigade, returned to\nAustralia 21.3.19.\nFate\n2.6.19 returned to Australia\non board ship Beltana.\n2.7.19 discharged 3rd\nMilitary\nDistrict.\nDied 5.11.50 Broomfield. Age\nat death, 60 years. Death\nRegistration 1950/22841. Place of\nburial: Creswick Cemetery on\n7.11.1950, where he was\nrecorded as a resident of\nBroomfield.\nRecognition\n• 1914–15 Star.\n• British War Medal.\n• Victory Medal.\n• Broomfield State School\nHonour Board.\n• RSL Virtual War\nMemorial.\nAvenue of Honour Tree E98\nAdditional Notes\nAt the time of his death, he\nwas recorded as being a\nresident of Broomfield and\noccupation grazier.\nHe started up a local football\nteam at Allendale and\nBroomfield in 1921.\nNotes\n24.6.16 disembarked at\nMarseilles.\n8.12.16 Mentioned in Orders\n“when his comrade's horses\nbecame reactive and bolted\nunder hostile shell fire, he\nturned back to rescue his\ncomrade who had been\nthrown and injured, and took\nhim to a safe place, then he\nreturned to recover the\nremaining horses.”\n3.1.17 admitted to 38th\nCasualty Clearing Station\nwith PUO (pyrexia of\nunknown origin). 6.1.17\nadmitted to 5th\nGeneral\nHospital Rouen, France.\n13.1.17 evacuated to\nEngland on board hospital\nship St. Patrick. 16.2.17\ndischarged and granted\nfurlough to 5.3.17.\n2.6.17 forfeited 14 days' pay\nfor being in the Sergeants'\nMess during prohibited\nhours. Punishment imposed\nby Lieutenant Colonel Lamb.\n3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser\nreported that Private\nRedman from Broomfield\nhad been a District\nVolunteer.\nAustralian National Archives\nrecords birth at Talbot.",
