    "additionalNotes": "",
    "notes": "11.11.16 arrived Plymouth,\nEngland.\n21.11.16 marched into 10th\nTraining Battalion, Hurdcott\nCamp.\n18.5.17 appointed Lance\nCorporal.\n7.6.17 Wounded in Action.\n(Gunshot wound to back.)\nTreated by 9th\nAustralian\nField Ambulance, then 2nd\nCasualty Clearing Station\nbefore being admitted to\n14th\nGeneral Hospital,\nWimereux, France. 22.7.17\nevacuated to England.\n24.7.17 stated he was dizzy\nand light-headed, but was\nassessed fit for training.\n8.11.17 rejoined Battalion in\nthe field.\n5.4.18 Wounded in Action.\n(Gunshot wounds to left hip\nand abdomen.) Died of\nWounds later that same day.\n3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser\nreported that Private\nWaddingham from Smeaton\nhad been a District\nVolunteer.\n31.12.18 Creswick Advertiser\npublished a list of all from\nthe Borough and Shire who\nhad been Killed in Action\nincluding Lance Corporal\nWaddingham.",
    "militaryMedalCitation": "",
    "fullProfile": "Waddingham, George Edward\nFamily\nBorn Smeaton 1892. Birth\nRegistration 1892/7968. Father:\nRobert Fowler Waddingham.\nMother: Elizabeth Mary\nJones. Married Edna Lillian\nWilson in 1920 at\nMerewether, NSW.\nEnlistment\nJoined 22.5.16. Service\nnumber: 2198. Rank: Private.\nRoll title: 39th\nInfantry\nBattalion, 1st\nto 6th\nReinforcements, (May to\nDecember 1916). Age on\nenlistment: 24 years 3\nmonths. Occupation: Miner.\nMarital status: Single.\nAddress at date of\nenlistment: Ararat. Religion:\nMethodist. Next of kin and\naddress: mother, Mrs.\nElizabeth Cowan, Smeaton\nPost Office. (Father\ndeceased).\nEmbarkation\nEmbarked Melbourne\n25.9.16 on ship A9 HMAT\nShropshire. Rate of pay per\ndiem before and after\nembarkation: Five Shillings.\nOriginal unit: 39th\nBattalion,\n3rd\nReinforcement.\nAustralian War Memorial\nEmbarkation Roll Number:\n23/56/2.\nFamily Military Connection\nBrother, Private Robert\nWilliam Waddingham, [No.\n1699], 35th\nInfantry\nBattalion, returned to\nAustralia 9.2.19.\nFate\n5.4.18 Died of Wounds while\nserving with the 39th\nInfantry\nBattalion, Buire, Somme\nSector, France. Age at death,\n26 years. Place of burial:\nQuerrieu British Cemetery,\n(Row A, Grave Number 9),\nFrance.\nCommemoration\nAustralian War Memorial,\nPanel Number 132, Roll of\nHonour.\nRecognition\n• British War Medal.\n• Victory Medal.\n• Memorial Plaque.\n• Memorial Scroll.\n• RSL Virtual War\nMemorial.\n• Smeaton State School\nHonour Board.\n• Kangaroo Hills State\nSchool Honour Board.\n• District of Ullina\nCommemorative Stone.\n• Ullina State School\nHonour Board.\n• Kooroocheang State\nSchool Honour Board.\nAvenue of Honour Tree W82\nNotes\n11.11.16 arrived Plymouth,\nEngland.\n21.11.16 marched into 10th\nTraining Battalion, Hurdcott\nCamp.\n18.5.17 appointed Lance\nCorporal.\n7.6.17 Wounded in Action.\n(Gunshot wound to back.)\nTreated by 9th\nAustralian\nField Ambulance, then 2nd\nCasualty Clearing Station\nbefore being admitted to\n14th\nGeneral Hospital,\nWimereux, France. 22.7.17\nevacuated to England.\n24.7.17 stated he was dizzy\nand light-headed, but was\nassessed fit for training.\n8.11.17 rejoined Battalion in\nthe field.\n5.4.18 Wounded in Action.\n(Gunshot wounds to left hip\nand abdomen.) Died of\nWounds later that same day.\n3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser\nreported that Private\nWaddingham from Smeaton\nhad been a District\nVolunteer.\n31.12.18 Creswick Advertiser\npublished a list of all from\nthe Borough and Shire who\nhad been Killed in Action\nincluding Lance Corporal\nWaddingham.",
