    "additionalNotes": "Australian National Archives\nrecords birth at Daylesford.\nEnlisted with his father's\nwritten consent.",
    "notes": "19.4.16 transferred to 59th\nBattalion at Serapeum,\nEgypt.\n26.5.16 penalised four days'\nfield punishment for being\nabsent from 5pm Parade at\nFerry Post, Egypt.\n18.6.16 embarked\nAlexandria, Egypt to join BEF\n[British Expeditionary Force]\nfor France onboard Kinfauns\nCastle. 29.6.16 arrived\nMarseilles.\n6.9.16 Enquiry determined\nsoldier died on 25.7.16 at\n30th\nGeneral Hospital Calais\nof wounds received in battle.\n(Gunshot wounds to left\nbuttock and right thigh.)\nBurial was performed by\nReverend M. R. Hardy.\n19.1.17 Creswick Advertiser\nreported that private Wade\nhad been Killed in Action.\n3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser\nreported that Private Wade\nfrom Rocky Lead had been a\nDistrict Volunteer.\n31.12.18 Creswick Advertiser\npublished a list of all from\nthe Borough and Shire who\nhad been Killed in Action\nincluding Private Wade.",
    "militaryMedalCitation": "",
    "fullProfile": "Wade, Sydney\nFamily\nBorn Rocky Lead 1898. Birth\nRegistration 1898/21876. Father:\nGeorge Thomas Wade.\nMother: Elizabeth Blair.\nEnlistment\nJoined 14.12.15 Ballarat.\nService number: 4951. Rank:\nPrivate. Roll title: 8th\nInfantry\nBattalion, 13th\nto 23rd\nReinforcements, (December\n1915 to November 1916).\nAge on enlistment: 18 years\n5 months. Occupation:\nLabourer. Marital status:\nSingle. Religion: Church of\nEngland. Next of kin and\naddress: father, Mr. Thomas\nWade, Rocky Lead Post\nOffice via Daylesford.\nEmbarkation\nEmbarked Melbourne 7.3.16\non ship A18 HMAT Wiltshire.\nRate of pay per diem before\nand after embarkation: Five\nShillings. Original unit: 8th\nBattalion, 15th\nReinforcement. Australian\nWar Memorial Embarkation\nRoll Number: 23/25/5.\nLater in this publication are more\ndetails about the ships that took\nindividuals overseas.\nAdditional Notes\nMiddle name recorded as\nVivian.\nFate\n26.7.16 Died of Wounds\nwhile serving with 8th\nInfantry Battalion in France.\nAge at death, 18 years. Place\nof burial: Calais Southern\nCemetery, (Plot E, Row 3,\nGrave Number 6), France.\nCommemoration\nAustralian War Memorial,\nPanel Number 169, Roll of\nHonour.\nRecognition\n• British War Medal.\n• Victory Medal.\n• Memorial Plaque.\n• Memorial Scroll.\n• RSL Virtual War\nMemorial.\nAvenue of Honour Tree W102\nAdditional Notes\nAustralian National Archives\nrecords birth at Daylesford.\nEnlisted with his father's\nwritten consent.\nNotes\n19.4.16 transferred to 59th\nBattalion at Serapeum,\nEgypt.\n26.5.16 penalised four days'\nfield punishment for being\nabsent from 5pm Parade at\nFerry Post, Egypt.\n18.6.16 embarked\nAlexandria, Egypt to join BEF\n[British Expeditionary Force]\nfor France onboard Kinfauns\nCastle. 29.6.16 arrived\nMarseilles.\n6.9.16 Enquiry determined\nsoldier died on 25.7.16 at\n30th\nGeneral Hospital Calais\nof wounds received in battle.\n(Gunshot wounds to left\nbuttock and right thigh.)\nBurial was performed by\nReverend M. R. Hardy.\n19.1.17 Creswick Advertiser\nreported that private Wade\nhad been Killed in Action.\n3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser\nreported that Private Wade\nfrom Rocky Lead had been a\nDistrict Volunteer.\n31.12.18 Creswick Advertiser\npublished a list of all from\nthe Borough and Shire who\nhad been Killed in Action\nincluding Private Wade.\nFamily Military Connection\nBrother, Private Albert Wade,\n[No. 1982], 23rd\nBattalion,\nreturned to Australia 2.9.16.\nBrother, Private George\nWade, [No. 1124], 4th\nLight\nHorse Regiment, returned to\nAustralia 21.1.16.",
