    "additionalNotes": "31.12.18 Creswick Advertiser\nreported all from the\nBorough and Shire who had\nbeen Wounded in Action\nduring WW1 and that\nincluded Private White.\nAustralian National Archives\nrecords birth at Ballarat.",
    "notes": "Prior Military Experience\nwith 71st\nInfantry.\n4.12.16 transferred from\nHMAT Port Lincoln to\nWillochre at Sierra Leone,\nWest Africa. 29.12.16\narrived Plymouth, England\nand immediately marched\ninto 10th\nTraining Battalion,\nDurrington Camp.\n15.1.17 at Durrington, given\n14 days' detention for failing\nto appear on parade. Penalty\ngiven by Major H. V. Carr.\n22.5.17 proceeded overseas\nto France via Southampton.\n20.9.17 Wounded in Action.\n(Shotgun wound to right\nthigh.) Treated by 11th\nCasualty Clearing Station\nbefore being evacuated to\nEngland on hospital ship HS\nNewhaven. Admitted to\nCoombe Lodge Hospital\nGreat Warley. 17.10.17\ntransferred to 2nd\nAuxiliary\nHospital Southall. 25.10.17\ndischarged to Depot at\nWeymouth.\n2.6.18 admitted to Bullford\nMilitary Hospital with\nabscess on right leg.\n3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser\nreported that Private White\nfrom Rocky Lead had been a\nDistrict Volunteer.",
    "militaryMedalCitation": "",
    "fullProfile": "White, Eric Claude\nFamily\nBorn Allendale 1898. Birth\nRegistration 1898/85. Father: John\nWhite. Mother: Margaret\nJane Croy. Married Jean\nGlover 1924. Marriage Registration\n1924/731.\nEnlistment\nJoined 28.8.16. Service\nnumber: 2415. Rank: Private.\nRoll title: 38th\nInfantry\nBattalion, 1st\nto 6th\nReinforcements, (June to\nDecember 1916). Age on\nenlistment: 18 years 8\nmonths. Occupation:\nLabourer. Marital status:\nSingle. Religion: Church of\nEngland. Next of kin and\naddress: Father, John White,\nAllendale.\nEmbarkation\nEmbarked Melbourne\n20.10.16 on ship A17 HMAT\nPort Lincoln. Rate of pay per\ndiem before and after\nembarkation: Five Shillings.\nOriginal unit: 38th\nBattalion,\n4th\nReinforcement.\nAustralian War Memorial\nEmbarkation Roll Number:\n23/55/2.\nLater in this publication are more\ndetails about the ships that took\nindividuals overseas.\nAdditional Notes\nAt the time of his death, he\nwas recorded as being a\nresident of Glen Waverley\nand occupation retired.\nFate\n21.12.18 returned to\nAustralia on board the ship\nMamari.\n6.3.19 discharged 3rd\nMilitary\nDistrict.\nDied 24.10.1972 Ashwood.\nAge at death, 74 years. Death\nRegistration 1972/25287. Cremated\nat Springvale Botanical\nCemetery on 25.10.1972.\nRecognition\n• British War Medal.\n• Victory Medal.\n• Allendale State School\nHonour Board.\n• Kooroocheang State\nSchool Honour Board.\n• RSL Virtual War\nMemorial.\nAvenue of Honour Tree W48\nAdditional Notes\n31.12.18 Creswick Advertiser\nreported all from the\nBorough and Shire who had\nbeen Wounded in Action\nduring WW1 and that\nincluded Private White.\nAustralian National Archives\nrecords birth at Ballarat.\nNotes\nPrior Military Experience\nwith 71st\nInfantry.\n4.12.16 transferred from\nHMAT Port Lincoln to\nWillochre at Sierra Leone,\nWest Africa. 29.12.16\narrived Plymouth, England\nand immediately marched\ninto 10th\nTraining Battalion,\nDurrington Camp.\n15.1.17 at Durrington, given\n14 days' detention for failing\nto appear on parade. Penalty\ngiven by Major H. V. Carr.\n22.5.17 proceeded overseas\nto France via Southampton.\n20.9.17 Wounded in Action.\n(Shotgun wound to right\nthigh.) Treated by 11th\nCasualty Clearing Station\nbefore being evacuated to\nEngland on hospital ship HS\nNewhaven. Admitted to\nCoombe Lodge Hospital\nGreat Warley. 17.10.17\ntransferred to 2nd\nAuxiliary\nHospital Southall. 25.10.17\ndischarged to Depot at\nWeymouth.\n2.6.18 admitted to Bullford\nMilitary Hospital with\nabscess on right leg.\n3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser\nreported that Private White\nfrom Rocky Lead had been a\nDistrict Volunteer.\nFamily Military Connection\nBrother, Private Orrie\nNicholson White, [No. 881],\n13th\nLight Horse Regiment,\nreturned to Australia 8.1.19.",
