    "notes": "Prior experience: three years\nwith Citizen Forces and three\nyears with Cadets.\n20.4.18 arrived Liverpool and\nproceeded to 12th\nTraining\nBattalion Codford Camp.\n10.7.18 forfeited five days'\npay for being absent without\npermission at Codford Camp.\nPenalty imposed by\nLieutenant Colonel A. P.\nImbay.\n15.7.18 proceeded overseas\nto France from Folkestone.\n8.8.18 Wounded in Action.\n(Gunshot wound to cheek.)\nInitially treated by 8th\nAustralian Field Ambulance,\nthen by 20th\nCasualty Control\nCentre. 15.8.18 evacuated\nto England where he was\nadmitted to Middlesex War\nHospital.\n23.9.18 proceeded on leave\nafter being discharged from\nHospital, then directed to\nreturn to Hurdcott Camp to\nprepare for return to France.\n21.1.19 appointed Driver.\n30.10.18 proceeded\noverseas to France from\nSouthampton.\n3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser\nreported that Private\nWilliams from Broomfield\nhad been a District\nVolunteer.",
    "militaryMedalCitation": "",
    "fullProfile": "Williams, George Daniel\nFamily\nBorn Broomfield 12.9.1896.\nBirth Registration 1896/25886.\nFather: John Williams.\nMother: Susan Green.\nMarried Rita Lillian James\n1924. Marriage Registration\n1924/2048.\nEnlistment\nJoined 8.11.17 Ballarat.\nService number: 3596. Rank:\nPrivate. Roll title: 46th\nInfantry Battalion, 10th\nReinforcements, (February\n1918). Age on enlistment: 21\nyears 2 months. Occupation:\nBlacksmith (Ballarat).\nAddress at date of\nenlistment: Broomfield.\nMarital status: Single.\nReligion: Presbyterian. Next\nof kin and address: father, J.\nWilliams, Ararat.\nEmbarkation\nEmbarked Melbourne\n28.2.18 on ship A71 HMAT\nNestor. Rate of pay per diem\nbefore embarkation: Six\nShillings. Original unit: 46th\nInfantry Battalion, 10th\nReinforcements.\nLater in this publication are more\ndetails about the ships that took\nindividuals overseas.\nAdditional Notes\nPrior to the war he had\nserved an apprenticeship of\nthree years with Blacksmith\nA. G. Barker of Kingston.\nFate\n1.7.19 returned to Australia\non the ship HT Frankfurt.\n26.9.19 discharged 3rd\nMilitary District.\nDied 1987. Death Registration\n1987/16224. Age at death, 91\nyears. Place of burial:\nCreswick Cemetery, where\nhe was recorded as a\nresident of Creswick.\nRecognition\n• British War Medal.\n• Victory Medal.\n• Hollinwood State School\nHonour Board.\n• Kooroocheang State\nSchool Honour Board.\n• Broomfield State School\nHonour Board.\n• RSL Virtual War\nMemorial.\nAvenue of Honour Tree W106\nAdditional Notes\n11.5.19 transferred to\nEngland to prepare for\nrepatriation to Australia.\nAt the time of his death, he\nwas recorded as being a\nresident of Creswick and\noccupation retired.\nNotes\nPrior experience: three years\nwith Citizen Forces and three\nyears with Cadets.\n20.4.18 arrived Liverpool and\nproceeded to 12th\nTraining\nBattalion Codford Camp.\n10.7.18 forfeited five days'\npay for being absent without\npermission at Codford Camp.\nPenalty imposed by\nLieutenant Colonel A. P.\nImbay.\n15.7.18 proceeded overseas\nto France from Folkestone.\n8.8.18 Wounded in Action.\n(Gunshot wound to cheek.)\nInitially treated by 8th\nAustralian Field Ambulance,\nthen by 20th\nCasualty Control\nCentre. 15.8.18 evacuated\nto England where he was\nadmitted to Middlesex War\nHospital.\n23.9.18 proceeded on leave\nafter being discharged from\nHospital, then directed to\nreturn to Hurdcott Camp to\nprepare for return to France.\n21.1.19 appointed Driver.\n30.10.18 proceeded\noverseas to France from\nSouthampton.\n3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser\nreported that Private\nWilliams from Broomfield\nhad been a District\nVolunteer.",
