    "notes": "19.1.17 disembarked\nPlymouth, England and\nimmediately marched into\n6th\nTraining Battalion at\nLarkhill Camp.\n20.2.17 admitted to Fargo\nMilitary Hospital, Salisbury\nEngland with bronchitis.\n18.3.17 discharged to 6th\nTraining Battalion, Larkhill\nCamp.\n15.4.17 admitted to Fargo\nMilitary Hospital, Salisbury\nEngland with influenza.\n12.5.17 discharged to depot.\n2.7.17 marched in from\nRollestone to Command\nDeport, Weymouth to await\nrepatriation to Australia.\n(Special order 448 dated\n1.7.17.)\n27.7.17 classified with a\nnervous debility and\ndeclared medically unfit for\nservice.\n1.11.17 granted a pension of\nfifteen shillings per fortnight.\nPension was cancelled on\n9.5.18.\n3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser\nreported that Private Wilson\nfrom Melbourne had been a\nDistrict Volunteer.\nAustralian National Archives\nrecords birth at Ballarat.",
    "militaryMedalCitation": "",
    "fullProfile": "Wilson, Alexander David\nFamily\nBorn Glengower 1887. Birth\nRegistration 1887/11519. Father:\nWilliam Wilson. Mother:\nCatherine McRae. Married\nGertrude Alexandra Bingley\nin 1924. Marriage Registration\n1924/5475.\nEnlistment\nJoined 25.10.16\nWarrnambool. Service\nnumber: 6474. Rank: Private.\nRoll title: 23rd\nInfantry\nBattalion, 13th\nto 18th\nReinforcements. (July to\nNovember 1916). Age on\nenlistment: 28 years 8\nmonths. Occupation: Farm\nLabourer (Warrnambool).\nMarital status: Single.\nReligion: Church of England.\nAddress at date of\nenlistment: Maroona,\nVictoria. Next of kin and\naddress: father, William\nWilson, Powlett Hill.\nEmbarkation\nEmbarked Melbourne\n23.11.16 on ship A20 HMAT\nHororata. Rate of pay per\ndiem before and after\nembarkation: Five Shillings.\nOriginal unit: 23rd\nBattalion\n18th\nReinforcement.\nAustralian War Memorial\nEmbarkation Roll Number:\n23/40/4.\nLater in this publication are more\ndetails about the ships that took\nindividuals overseas.\nFate\n27.7.17 returned to Australia\non board the ship\nDemosthenes.\n31.10.17 discharged 3rd\nMilitary District as medically\nunfit.\nDied 1967 Ararat. Age at\ndeath, 80 years. Death\nRegistration 1967/15757. Place of\nburial: Ararat Cemetery on\n19.7.67.\nRecognition\n• British War Medal.\n• Dean State School\nHonour Board.\n• RSL Virtual War\nMemorial.\nAvenue of Honour Tree E115\nAdditional Notes\nAt the time of his death, he\nwas recorded as being a\nresident of Ararat and\noccupation retired station\nhand.\nHe grew up at the family\nfarm at Powlett Hill.\nAlso known as Alex.\nNotes\n19.1.17 disembarked\nPlymouth, England and\nimmediately marched into\n6th\nTraining Battalion at\nLarkhill Camp.\n20.2.17 admitted to Fargo\nMilitary Hospital, Salisbury\nEngland with bronchitis.\n18.3.17 discharged to 6th\nTraining Battalion, Larkhill\nCamp.\n15.4.17 admitted to Fargo\nMilitary Hospital, Salisbury\nEngland with influenza.\n12.5.17 discharged to depot.\n2.7.17 marched in from\nRollestone to Command\nDeport, Weymouth to await\nrepatriation to Australia.\n(Special order 448 dated\n1.7.17.)\n27.7.17 classified with a\nnervous debility and\ndeclared medically unfit for\nservice.\n1.11.17 granted a pension of\nfifteen shillings per fortnight.\nPension was cancelled on\n9.5.18.\n3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser\nreported that Private Wilson\nfrom Melbourne had been a\nDistrict Volunteer.\nAustralian National Archives\nrecords birth at Ballarat.",
