    "id": 238,
    "treeNum": "E88",
    "name": "Wood, Alfred Percival",
    "gender": "Male",
    "rank": "Private",
    "role": "Soldier",
    "location": "Creswick / the June quarter",
    "service": "Joined 3.2.15 Kingston.\nService number: 914. Rank:\nPrivate. Roll title: 22nd\nInfantry Battalion (May\n1915). Age on enlistment: 19\nyears. Occupation: Labourer\n(Kingston). Marital status:\nSingle. Religion: Methodist.\nNext of kin and address:\nfather, John Wood, 14\nMafeking Avenue, Plaistow,\nEast Ham, Essex, England. Embarked Melbourne\n10.5.15 on ship A38 HMAT\nUlysses. Rate of pay per diem\nbefore and after\nembarkation: Five Shillings.\nOriginal unit: 22nd\nBattalion,\nD Company. Australian War\nMemorial Embarkation Roll\nNumber: 23/39/1.\nLater in this publication are more\ndetails about the ships that took\nindividuals overseas.",
    "family": "Born in the June quarter\n1897, Plaistowe, Essex\nEngland. Father: John Wood.\nMother: Emma Glen.",
    "enlistment": "Joined 3.2.15 Kingston.\nService number: 914. Rank:\nPrivate. Roll title: 22nd\nInfantry Battalion (May\n1915). Age on enlistment: 19\nyears. Occupation: Labourer\n(Kingston). Marital status:\nSingle. Religion: Methodist.\nNext of kin and address:\nfather, John Wood, 14\nMafeking Avenue, Plaistow,\nEast Ham, Essex, England.",
    "embarkation": "Embarked Melbourne\n10.5.15 on ship A38 HMAT\nUlysses. Rate of pay per diem\nbefore and after\nembarkation: Five Shillings.\nOriginal unit: 22nd\nBattalion,\nD Company. Australian War\nMemorial Embarkation Roll\nNumber: 23/39/1.\nLater in this publication are more\ndetails about the ships that took\nindividuals overseas.",
    "fate": "15.7.19 returned to Australia\non board the ship HT Port\nMacQuarie.\n25.7.19 discharged 3rd\nMilitary District.\nNo marriage or death\nregistration for Victoria.",
    "recognition": "• Military Medal.\n• 1914–15 Star.\n• British War Medal.\n• Victory Medal.\n• RSL Virtual War\nMemorial.\nAvenue of Honour Tree E88\nMilitary Medal Citation:\n“After his Company\nCommander was killed, he\ntook charge of the Coy\n(Company) HQ and led them\nto the objective, sending\nword of his actions to the\nCoy. Second in\nCommand…He then\nvolunteered to carry\nimportant messages through\nthe heavy barrage of high\nexplosives and gas…”",
    "commemoration": "",
    "warService": "",
    "nursingService": "",
    "familyConnection": "",
    "additionalNotes": "",
    "notes": "20.5.16 penalised 28 days'\nfield punishment for\nbreaking from Platoon\nwithout permission while on\nthe march and for failing to\nreport for fatigue duty in the\ntrenches.\n20.7.17 admitted to hospital\nwith PUO (pyrexia of\nunknown origin).\n14.8.17 admitted to 5th\nSouthern General Hospital\nwith trench fever.\n12.11.17 at Perham Downs\nEngland, sentenced to 49\ndays' detention and 25 days'\nloss of pay for being absent\nwithout permission.\nDetention served at Lewis\nDetention Barracks Sutton\nVeny Camp.\n3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser\nreported that Private Wood\nfrom Kingston had been a\nDistrict Volunteer.\n3.7.19 Awarded Military\nMedal for most conspicuous\ngallantry and devotion to\nduty during the attack near\nGeneve east of Peronne on\n3rd\n/4th\nOctober 1918.\nNot recorded on the 1901\ncensus, so one presumes he\nwas not in Australia at that\npoint in time.",
    "militaryMedalCitation": "",
    "fullProfile": "Wood, Alfred Percival\nFamily\nBorn in the June quarter\n1897, Plaistowe, Essex\nEngland. Father: John Wood.\nMother: Emma Glen.\nEnlistment\nJoined 3.2.15 Kingston.\nService number: 914. Rank:\nPrivate. Roll title: 22nd\nInfantry Battalion (May\n1915). Age on enlistment: 19\nyears. Occupation: Labourer\n(Kingston). Marital status:\nSingle. Religion: Methodist.\nNext of kin and address:\nfather, John Wood, 14\nMafeking Avenue, Plaistow,\nEast Ham, Essex, England.\nEmbarkation\nEmbarked Melbourne\n10.5.15 on ship A38 HMAT\nUlysses. Rate of pay per diem\nbefore and after\nembarkation: Five Shillings.\nOriginal unit: 22nd\nBattalion,\nD Company. Australian War\nMemorial Embarkation Roll\nNumber: 23/39/1.\nLater in this publication are more\ndetails about the ships that took\nindividuals overseas.\nFate\n15.7.19 returned to Australia\non board the ship HT Port\nMacQuarie.\n25.7.19 discharged 3rd\nMilitary District.\nNo marriage or death\nregistration for Victoria.\nRecognition\n• Military Medal.\n• 1914–15 Star.\n• British War Medal.\n• Victory Medal.\n• RSL Virtual War\nMemorial.\nAvenue of Honour Tree E88\nMilitary Medal Citation:\n“After his Company\nCommander was killed, he\ntook charge of the Coy\n(Company) HQ and led them\nto the objective, sending\nword of his actions to the\nCoy. Second in\nCommand…He then\nvolunteered to carry\nimportant messages through\nthe heavy barrage of high\nexplosives and gas…”\nNotes\n20.5.16 penalised 28 days'\nfield punishment for\nbreaking from Platoon\nwithout permission while on\nthe march and for failing to\nreport for fatigue duty in the\ntrenches.\n20.7.17 admitted to hospital\nwith PUO (pyrexia of\nunknown origin).\n14.8.17 admitted to 5th\nSouthern General Hospital\nwith trench fever.\n12.11.17 at Perham Downs\nEngland, sentenced to 49\ndays' detention and 25 days'\nloss of pay for being absent\nwithout permission.\nDetention served at Lewis\nDetention Barracks Sutton\nVeny Camp.\n3.12.18 Creswick Advertiser\nreported that Private Wood\nfrom Kingston had been a\nDistrict Volunteer.\n3.7.19 Awarded Military\nMedal for most conspicuous\ngallantry and devotion to\nduty during the attack near\nGeneve east of Peronne on\n3rd\n/4th\nOctober 1918.\nNot recorded on the 1901\ncensus, so one presumes he\nwas not in Australia at that\npoint in time.",
